WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.025
OK.

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:07.940
We'll continue.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.090
There was a couple of things
I wanted to point out here

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.250
that we already saw a couple.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.930
So the idea here is you create
a penalty function, which

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.240
basically describes how you
feel, how irritated are you

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.610
with a residual of
a certain value.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:29.780
So these are residuals
here, and then this

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.400
is basically your irritation.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.200
And what you're going
to do is actually

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.470
minimize your total
irritation by minimizing

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.490
the sum of these things.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.280
And then you can interpret
these all sorts of ways.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.260
Your standard thing
would be a quadratic,

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.110
that's been a tradition for
a couple of hundred years,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:50.000
and also has analytical
solutions in some simple cases.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.380
But then we have some others
here like this deadzone-linear.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.800
And these are interesting
because they have a kink here.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.990
And what we observed last
time was actually interesting,

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:03.770
it was this.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:08.370
It was that when you
have a kink here, you

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.660
find a whole bunch of
values right at the kink.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.770
Notice that that's also true
for the L1 solution as well.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:22.900
So the L1 solution says
you don't like residuals--

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.490
in fact, one way to analyze
what an L1 penalty function is

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.620
is that, for large
residuals, it's

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.910
relatively chill, at least,
for a convex penalty.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.550
Because it's a lot
smaller than a quadratic

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.680
when a residual is large because
a quadratic is large squared.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.120
Conversely, for small
residuals, something

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.700
like an L1 or something
with a sharp point

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.030
is actually not chill.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.140
It gets very upset with--
it gets relatively upset

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.770
with small residuals whereas,
for example, a square penalty

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.542
is not, is fine.

00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:03.000
And I'm just-- there's no more
to what I'm saying than just

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.470
this, so don't try to over--

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.178
I mean, you can-- if
you wanted to people

00:02:06.178 --> 00:02:08.220
have actually attempted
to make this extremely

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.083
mathematical and so on.

00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:12.000
It's a bit silly because
I think it's actually

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.420
very interesting to actually
just see how this works.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.000
OK.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.150
That is actually something--
especially this top one, which

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.150
is the L1 thing, here you see
that a whole bunch of penalties

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.740
are 0.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.240
And, in fact, this
is a theme you're

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.120
going to see-- you may already
have seen it in some statistics

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.500
and machine learning classes.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.970
And basically what
people say is that L1

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.410
is a sparsifier, meaning
that when you take

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.500
an L1 norm of something in
an optimization problem,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.950
it is often the case
that at the solution,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.800
the argument has a
lot of 0 entries.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.810
That's the case here, OK?

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.570
And so that's just
a common phenomenon,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.540
and it's got tons
of uses, including

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.040
a lot of practical uses.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.350
We'll get to it in
machine learning,

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.650
there's just tons of them,
but it comes up in engineering

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.390
and has been used actually
now for several decades.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.105
So OK.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.080
I guess this is the-- we
looked at the Huber penalty

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.390
that that focuses on large
residuals, and the fact

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.750
that a Huber penalty is like
this Frankenstein penalty that

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.570
blends together a quadratic
for small residuals

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.940
with, basically,
something that looks

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.720
like an L1 penalty for large.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.930
And so you might say that
a Huber penalty matches

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.500
least squares for small
residuals, which is to say

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.120
that it's relatively chill.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.790
Not as chill as a deadzone,
that's as chill as you can get.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.770
That's like, well, OK.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.490
But for large residuals,
a Huber penalty actually

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:51.820
looks more like an L1 thing,
which is, for a convex penalty,

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.190
as chill as you can get.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.780
So OK.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.180
We looked at least-norm
problems where,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.950
basically, the same story
happens, but now I can--

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.250
you can guess a lot of stuff.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:14.070
If, for example, X is a sequence
of forces that you apply--

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.220
let's see-- or yeah, that
you apply to something.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.890
Let's say Ax equals
B says, make me

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:23.610
a trajectory of forces to
put every 20 milliseconds

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.360
on this rigid body to
move it from this position

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.950
to that position.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.620
Let's just say that's what X is.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.980
If I use an L1 norm here,
I want someone to guess.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.250
Which is, by the way, a
first order approximation

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.470
of fuel use, at
least, for rockets.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.700
So somebody want to
make a guess as to what

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.700
it's going to look like.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.000
What is X going to
be based on what

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.485
we were just talking about?

00:04:50.485 --> 00:04:51.790
It's going to be sparse.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.860
It's going to be sparse, yeah.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.170
And by the way, if
you go and watch

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.910
videos of satellites maneuvering
or rockets maneuvering,

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.030
that's exactly what
you'll see if you watch.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.780
Because you'll actually
see puffs come out

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.170
of the thrusters, and there's
little bursts, and it'll--

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.170
you'll see 1, 2, 3,
and then just nothing.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.112
And then some other
thruster you'll

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:15.320
get like two bursts out of
it or something like that.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.230
So that's exactly
right, they're sparse.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.660
Everybody got this?

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.710
So that's a theme.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:23.530
Don't worry, you're going to
see it basically from now on.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:27.890
And it's used in a whole
lot of other applications,

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:30.670
not just-- well, that's one.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:35.060
So anyway, OK,
we'll get to that.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.410
OK.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.480
We talked about
regularized approximation

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.320
last time as, basically,
a bicriterion problem,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.590
where you have two criteria.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:44.870
And, of course, you
could extend it and have

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.290
three and things like that.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:50.690
And I think we looked at an
example of that, not this one,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.743
but optimal input design.

00:05:51.743 --> 00:05:52.910
We looked at this last time.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.290
And this is very typical.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.290
This is a pure quadratic
problem, so it's not that--

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.212
I mean, it's very simple.

00:05:59.212 --> 00:06:00.920
You don't need the
material of this class

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.030
to solve this problem.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.410
But it looks
something like this.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.680
You're designing
an input, which is

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.980
an input to some system which
is given by a convolution,

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.970
or in dialect I would say
it's a linear time invariant

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.630
system that would be like EE
and maybe mechanical engineering

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.520
dialect for what this is.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.070
So the output is the input
convolved with something.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.470
I guess in fancy
language you would

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.960
say it's the convolution
kernel, that would be something

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.280
that everyone would understand.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.590
But if you're
exclusively with people

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.800
who speak this EE,
ME dialect, you

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.830
would say something like
h is the impulse response.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.950
So I'm just mentioning
that different groups

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.770
have different names
for these things.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.270
OK.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.520
So here we have
three objectives.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.750
The first one is we want to
track some desired trajectory.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.850
We want the input
small, roughly we're

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.950
making it L2 norm small squared,
and we want the input variation

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:02.590
to be small.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.890
So we want the difference
between u t plus 1

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.410
and ut to be relatively small.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.220
And so what we'll do
is we will minimize

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.750
a weighted sum of them.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.863
These are positive weights,
the delta and the eta,

00:07:14.863 --> 00:07:16.530
that weight the
different things, right?

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.220
And then the way--

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.970
in fact, people in control in
other areas talk about this

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:21.990
is they would say--

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.300
they would say that
the delta and the eta

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.680
are knobs, design
knobs that will allows

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.170
you to shape, to make
you look like what

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.700
you want it to look like.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.520
Everybody got that?

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.020
Right.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.900
So I guess in machine learning
you'd call them hyperparameters

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.790
which are things you wiggle
to change your model,

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.500
to make it less
sensitive to something

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:44.460
or something like that.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.645
Everybody got that?

00:07:45.645 --> 00:07:47.520
Unlike in machine learning
where you actually

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.470
have rather good ways to defend
choices of delta and eta,

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.080
in a ton of applications,
you don't, right?

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.570
So I mean, you do
kind of, right?

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.690
So in control, you do
something like pick some values

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.880
and say, ooh, that's nice.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.760
Saves a lot of fuel, but the
ride is too rough, right?

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.012
And then you would
wiggle these things,

00:08:08.012 --> 00:08:10.470
multiply them, one of them or
two of them by a factor of 2,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.360
divide one by a
factor of 2, rerun it

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.430
and you'd say, OK, that looks
we're only using about 5%.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.347
We only lost a few percent
in fuel efficiency.

00:08:17.347 --> 00:08:19.513
But it's a lot smoother
ride or something like that.

00:08:19.513 --> 00:08:20.850
It would be things like this.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.280
So, OK.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.550
And we looked actually at some
examples of how that works.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:31.110
And the example actually shows
what the whole idea here is.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.284
And it's identical to
the idea, by the way,

00:08:33.284 --> 00:08:35.159
in regularization and
machine learning, which

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:36.960
we'll talk about later.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.690
It's identical and
what it is based on

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.360
is you can actually
sometimes get away

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:49.170
with not a huge loss in
your primary objective while

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.440
at the same time actually
getting a substantial reduction

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.420
in your secondary objectives.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:57.570
So this is a, a lot smoother
than that and also b,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.550
a lot smaller.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.740
I mean, if this
is not good enough

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.360
tracking error,
this versus that,

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.450
then this is all
theoretical, right?

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.700
But if you say like,
oh, that's fine.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:12.190
That's all I wanted
this thing to do--

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:13.180
oh, and I think--

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.692
I couldn't remember
the name in control

00:09:15.692 --> 00:09:17.150
or mechanical
engineering for this.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:18.460
It's called a doublet.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.260
Is that-- someone
told me anyway.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.950
It's something like a doublet
motion or something like that.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.950
That's what it is when
something goes like this.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.650
It goes over here, over
there, and then back.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.320
So OK, all right.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.310
Does this make sense?

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.750
And now I'm going to
ask you a question.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:38.830
What would happen
if I change this

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:42.580
to the sum of the
absolute values?

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.650
You just guess.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.750
I mean, three lines of
Python and you would know.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:48.910
But just if someone
could just make a guess.

00:09:51.568 --> 00:09:52.860
What do you think would happen?

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.210
Would it be sparsity?

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.820
Yeah, but I don't think
u was sparse at all.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.890
Like here let's go look at u.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:09:59.940
Sorry.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:00.930
Here's u.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.950
Well, that's not true.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.480
I shouldn't have spoke, right?

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.730
It does look like it doesn't
do anything for 40 steps.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.110
It would be sparser, right?

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.280
What if I changed this to be
the sum of the absolute values?

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.892
What would you get?

00:10:15.892 --> 00:10:17.475
I mean, you're just
guessing here so--

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:22.040
what's that?

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:22.970
Sparse derivative?

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:23.793
Sparse derivative.

00:10:23.793 --> 00:10:24.710
It's not a derivative.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:25.710
It's a first difference.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:26.990
Yes, sparse first difference.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:28.490
What does it mean
if something has--

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.520
if a time series has a
sparse first difference, what

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:32.330
does that mean?

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:33.380
More smooth.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:35.750
It's what?

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:36.690
More smooth.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:37.400
It's what?

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.110
More smooth.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.310
I can't hear.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:41.360
More smooth.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.860
More smooth.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.870
I don't think that's
very smooth, no.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.300
I mean, it's smooth in
a certain sense, right?

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.630
In the sense that the L1 norm of
the first difference is small.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.410
I don't know.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:52.250
Yeah?

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:53.060
Piecewise.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.710
Piecewise what?

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:57.350
Kind of constant and when
it changes, it's constant.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.030
It's piecewise constant, right?

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.970
If I give you a time
series and I tell you

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.870
that the first
differences are sparse,

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:07.820
then it means that most
of the time it's 0.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:09.800
And that means most
of the time the value

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.220
is equal to the previous value.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.480
So it would encourage piecewise
constant inputs, right?

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.160
So make sense?

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:20.000
Yeah, so that's actually used
in chemical engineering plants

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.670
when they're planning out
changes to inputs and things

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:24.330
like that.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:27.210
They actually put
a term that looks

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:28.800
like this with absolute values.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.960
And they even have
some great name

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.570
for that like I
think they call--

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.190
what happens then
is you get things

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.530
that says you're
going to run 3.5 hours

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.380
and then you're going to
increase the feed rate to this,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:41.070
right?

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:43.832
And then 3 hours
after that, you're

00:11:43.832 --> 00:11:45.540
going to turn up the
power to this heater

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:46.530
or something like that.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.020
And they call those
deltas which is--

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.780
I mean, it's dialect, but
it's kind of cool that

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.630
that's-- and it's done exactly
by solving actually quadratic

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:55.530
programs.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:57.990
Actually, it looks
exactly like that.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:02.160
But with an absolute value here.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.355
And the whole idea here should
be kind of obvious, right?

00:12:05.355 --> 00:12:09.540
We make-- you can
design these penalty

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.970
terms, and objective
terms, and things

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:15.630
like that to get something
that you want, right?

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.110
Like some kind of sparsity
or something like that.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:24.480
OK, next up is another
very simple problem.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.533
It's called signal
reconstruction.

00:12:26.533 --> 00:12:27.950
It didn't stop
people from writing

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:29.010
a thousand papers on it.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.370
But still, here it is.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:32.580
And it goes like this.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.580
So I give you a
corrupted signal.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.730
And then what you
want to do is form a--

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:39.440
you want to form an
approximation of it,

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.440
or an uncorrupted
or cleaned signal,

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:43.908
or a reconstructed signal.

00:12:43.908 --> 00:12:45.200
We're going to call that x hat.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:46.280
That's our variable.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.140
X corrupted is given, right?

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.950
So that would be
something like that.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:54.050
And then what
you're also given is

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.330
you're given phi is
something like it's

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:58.550
a regularization function
or a smoothing objective.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:04.490
So it basically says that it
says that the larger phi is,

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:10.700
the less plausible x hat is as
what it really-- what you're

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.770
trying to reconstruct, right?

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.840
So simple things would be things
like a quadratic smoother.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.840
This would be the one we
were just talked about.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:19.400
It's an L1 smoother.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.260
You could take the
third difference

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.240
and take the L1 difference.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.250
You could put a
dead zone penalty

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:27.510
on the second difference.

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.550
I mean, it just there's
just a lot of stuff

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.040
you can do with this, OK?

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:35.700
So this is sort of the idea.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:36.690
Now, by the way--

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:42.230
OK, so let's just take a
look at how this works.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:45.540
So here's just a kind of a
classic example for how you

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.890
would do signal reconstruction.

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.220
So here's an original signal.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:51.830
And you can see it's smooth
or something like that.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:55.820
And now here we add
some noise to it.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:57.490
And it looks like that, right?

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:01.840
And now we simply do-- this is
with quadratic smoothing, which

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.360
by the way, turns
out to be linear.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.480
And it turns out to be
exactly classical smoothing

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:10.300
and whatever, all the
stuff you would learn

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:11.260
and stuff like that.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.050
That's what it is.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:13.150
So that's what that is.

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.910
And it's basically
the smoother is then--

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:17.050
I'll go into dialect--

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.915
a low pass filter is what
people would say, OK?

00:14:19.915 --> 00:14:21.790
So and someone would
say, what are you doing?

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:24.340
And you say, I'm filtering
out the high frequency noise

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:30.310
here to estimate the signal
which has lots of low frequency

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:31.240
content.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:35.590
OK, so that's quasi dialect for
saying this thing is smooth,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.980
and the noise added
to it is much rougher.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:41.900
OK, I mean, you could say that
in other dialects too, right?

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:43.460
Certainly, in time
series dialect,

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:45.470
you would be able to
say something like that.

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:47.090
I don't exactly
speak that dialect,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:49.340
so I'm not going to even
attempt, but you could.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.350
OK, and then here are
some examples, right?

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:54.440
So you have a trade off
where you're trading off

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.450
two things, like how
far do you deviate

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.070
from what was given to you?

00:14:58.070 --> 00:14:59.660
That's this term.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:03.260
And the second term is how
small is this quadratic,

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:04.490
or how big is this quadratic?

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.830
Or in this case,
quadratic smoothing cost.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:12.110
And you can see this is with--

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:14.510
this thing is
relatively high here,

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:18.560
this one, the
quadratic component.

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:21.320
And this is something like
you might guess, I guess, just

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.900
by eyeball you might say
that's not enough smoothing,

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:27.693
which means you should--
it depends which one you're

00:15:27.693 --> 00:15:28.610
putting the weight on.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:30.020
Probably you're putting
the weight on this.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.850
And it says you should
probably decrease

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.920
the weight on the
smoothing regularizer,

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.595
then you might get
something like this.

00:15:35.595 --> 00:15:37.220
And then you might
say over here, well,

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:40.490
that's already too smoothed
or something like that, right?

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:47.540
So here, by the way, you
can use an obvious method

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:50.390
to actually, in a
very principled way,

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.070
select the amount of smoothing.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:54.230
You just do cross-validation
here, right?

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:56.520
So I think a lot of
people who do this,

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:58.580
don't do that, which
I find bizarre.

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:01.417
But nevertheless-- because
it's in your first class

00:16:01.417 --> 00:16:03.500
on statistics and machine
learning-- nevertheless,

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:04.490
you could.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.250
And you do that by
doing the following.

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:10.280
What you do to do that,
I'll just explain it,

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:14.720
is over here in this
one, you take the x,

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:17.180
the one that you're
given, x corrupted.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:21.330
And you extend this formulation
to allow it to have NAs in it,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.790
so unknown, like
unknown measurements,

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:29.898
like I didn't get a measurement
for index 37, 52, 53, 62, 70,

00:16:29.898 --> 00:16:30.690
anyway you do that.

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:31.950
And it's actually
extremely simple

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.825
if you make this a sum
of squares over those.

00:16:33.825 --> 00:16:37.320
But the x hat is going
to be a full one.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.020
It doesn't have any NAs in
it, if you're following me.

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:41.430
NA meaning not a number, right?

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:44.280
Or NAN, I think that would be
the other way to say it, right?

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:47.100
Or question mark, or whatever,
or missing data, right?

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:48.990
So then you do this--

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:52.980
now, once you extend this,
which is unbelievably simple

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.460
to handle missing data, now
you can do cross validation.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:17:00.150
And you do that by saying thanks
for your data, 10% is missing.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.670
You randomly yank
out another 10%

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:05.910
of the data which you
knew but isn't missing,

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:07.319
and you pretend it's missing.

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:09.750
Now, you do signal
reconstruction

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:13.770
and you try this thing and
someone at the next desk

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:16.829
says, oh, you should try
Huber, try whatever, try

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:18.150
Huber on the third difference.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:19.233
And you're like, OK, dude.

00:17:19.233 --> 00:17:20.730
And you try these things.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:24.130
Every time you do that,
you can make a prediction,

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:29.280
which is x hat of the values
that you actually observed

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.350
but pretended you didn't.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:32.460
Everybody see that?

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:34.590
And then we can say
thanks for your suggesting

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:38.460
of using a Huber penalty
on the third difference.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:41.490
Just for the record, it
did a lot worse than mine.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.770
OK, so everyone, if you
follow that, that's fine.

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:47.610
And if you didn't,
it's probably OK.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:52.800
So I mean, certainly not part
of the class, this class, OK.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:18:03.750
Now, when your penalty is an L1
norm on the first difference,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:04.980
that's actually got a name.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.710
And this is like just
completely standard math.

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:09.690
It's the total variation, right?

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:12.840
So it's the-- it's
actually-- well, literally,

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:15.840
it's in continuous time
it's like the integral

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.850
of the absolute value
of the derivative

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:19.170
or something like that, right?

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:20.970
And so this is the
analog of that.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.490
And as we actually
reasoned earlier

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:25.830
when you have a
penalty, which is

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.680
like an L1 norm on
a difference, you

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:34.920
should be expecting the solution
to have a difference, which

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:39.870
is sparse which corresponds
to a piecewise constant

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:41.220
approximation, right?

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.860
And so here's an example
here where here's

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.530
this original thing, right?

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:48.460
And here's the corrupted one.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.900
And I'll come back and describe
how people in some dialects

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:53.905
would describe this.

00:18:53.905 --> 00:18:55.030
But the point is that's it.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:56.095
Now we're going to do L1.

00:18:58.630 --> 00:19:00.760
OK, sorry, this is
quadratic, right?

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:04.000
So in quadratic, you see you
do a little bit of smoothing.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.350
And good news is like the
noise has gone way down.

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:10.550
Bad news, you can't
really see it here.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:12.970
But what was actually a
transition in one period

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:16.870
up here, these sharp things,
were actually are now smoothed

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.730
and they go down
over 10, 20 periods.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:19.960
That's over here.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.390
When I put this amount
on it, good news,

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:23.770
this noise is attenuated more.

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:26.710
But now you can see it's
really quite smooth, right?

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:29.860
And then this is
very smooth, right?

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:34.300
So if you wanted to
preserve these shifts,

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:37.780
then you would use instead
of a quadratic smoother,

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:42.550
you would use an
L1 smoother, OK?

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:44.430
And this sort of makes sense.

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.200
I was going to say like
if you know about audio,

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.400
this is just applying
a low pass filter.

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:51.640
And what you're losing are,
for example, the attack

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.080
of a drumbeat, right?

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:53.980
And it would just--

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.710
good news, the noise
would be attenuated.

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:59.590
Bad news, the drums are muffled.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:01.990
I mean, that was rough but
actually correct, right?

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:03.220
What I just said, OK.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:07.430
So now we do a total
variation smoothing.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.690
So total variation
smoothing goes like this.

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.430
This is maybe not quite enough.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:14.950
This might be about right.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.710
But the cool part is if
you take a look at it,

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:21.460
it actually has preserved these
sharp these sharp shock OK

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:23.800
shocks sorry that's from
econometrics, right?

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:26.200
So but so there's a
lot-- this comes up

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:29.260
in lots and lots of fields
all with different dialects

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.210
and names for everything, right?

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:33.100
And then this one is
sort of interesting.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:35.350
You might say that's a
little bit over regularized

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:37.600
and what you can
see here is it's

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.450
going towards what we guessed
was going to happen, right?

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:42.158
That you're going to
get something that's

00:20:42.158 --> 00:20:43.480
piecewise constant, right?

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:46.000
And it would look like this.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.110
OK, everybody,
everybody got this?

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:49.850
And so there's no
mystery here it

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:52.900
just this is it just
follows from knowing

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:56.860
about what happens with L1
norms and things like that.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:57.400
Let's see.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:58.900
I should probably
make some other--

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.660
yeah.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:02.510
Yeah, so you get the same thing.

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:04.840
You get total variation
denoising, for example,

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:06.550
in images.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.410
That's actually
super interesting.

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:10.690
It's actually not sum of--

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:12.670
you can do a sum
of absolute values.

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:14.470
You can take an image, right?

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:18.280
Let's say represented
by a matrix, right?

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:20.290
And you could do something
like this, right?

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:23.620
You could have i j plus 1.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:33.220
And then you would have Xij
minus Xi minus 1 and j, right?

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:35.620
And so you can take
the L1 norm of that

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:40.420
and then sum it over all
pixels in your image, right?

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:43.780
This is sort of like-- this is
a very crude idea of the space

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:47.790
gradient in the image.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:48.780
OK.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.820
So it turns out a very
good thing to do there.

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:52.530
If you actually take
the L1 norm of this,

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:53.947
you actually end
up with artifacts

00:21:53.947 --> 00:21:55.350
that are aligned with the axes.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.570
But if you just simply do this--

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:01.920
is a generalization
of an absolute value.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:03.900
If you take this
objective here--

00:22:06.730 --> 00:22:10.050
let me draw that a
little bit better.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.480
By the way, this is not squared.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:13.620
It is not squared.

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:15.630
It's just the sum of the norms.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:17.310
Everybody got that?

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.050
That's called total variation.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:22.740
That's a total variation
penalty for images.

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:27.130
That works, like, actually
really well, like, quite well.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.620
I mean, if you want to get
into fancier ones, you can.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.170
But this is like--
as a first cut,

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:32.770
this is actually
really quite good.

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:36.720
And what happens there
is it preserves--

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.360
it's going to preserve
sharp boundaries.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:41.790
Obviously, there's boundaries
if I'm looking over there.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:45.210
Some pixels are the podium, and
some is the chair behind it,

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:46.690
and there's a sharp boundary.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:51.430
And if I were to do this,
smoothing with this,

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:52.270
I'm just, again--

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.100
I'm just doing a
linear smoothing

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:57.130
with a kernel that
goes down like that,

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:59.530
and it's going to blur things.

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:02.050
Everybody-- I mean, you
don't have to follow this,

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:03.670
but if you know some of the
stuff I'm talking about,

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:04.300
that's great.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.640
So this is total variation--

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:11.110
that people refer to that
as total variation image

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:14.415
denoising.

00:23:14.415 --> 00:23:15.660
Have people seeing this--

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:17.820
who has seen this before?

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:19.230
Somewhere, OK, cool.

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.010
In where?

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:21.120
In what kind of--

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:24.300
Pressure jumps
across shockwaves.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:25.590
But we have a similar--

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.090
Yeah, so this preserves.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.630
What happens if I use excessive
regularization of this

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:31.750
on an image?

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:33.990
So I have a black
and white image.

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.970
I give it to you,
and you say I just

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:40.350
learned about total variation,
whatever, denoising.

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:45.330
And you use too much
of this regularization.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:47.620
What do you get?

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:48.790
Just guess.

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:50.200
I think everyone can guess.

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:50.800
Blur.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:51.300
What?

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:52.480
Blur

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:53.890
Same color?

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:54.700
Ooh.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:55.360
Yeah, OK.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:56.110
Sorry, yes.

00:23:56.110 --> 00:23:57.000
Not a blur.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:57.520
No, no.

00:23:57.520 --> 00:23:58.640
I don't think you--

00:23:58.640 --> 00:23:59.140
OK.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:02.200
If you-- OK, oh,
by the way, if I

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:04.330
add too much like
L1 regularization,

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:05.410
what's the limit of that?

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.110
What happens when I
add an enormous amount?

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:09.070
It's 0.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:11.740
It's 0, which is indeed
very sparse, right?

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:13.040
You have to admit that, right?

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:13.540
OK.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:16.960
So yes, if it was way
too high, it would say,

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:18.070
here's your new image.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:21.340
And the entire thing is just
one some grayscale value,

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:22.630
which is kind of the--

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:24.980
OK, everybody got that?

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:25.480
OK.

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:28.900
Let's crank it down to
something reasonable.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:30.860
And what do you think happens?

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:35.200
I mean, it's something
like this phenomenon here.

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:37.050
what do you think--
but in an image, what

00:24:37.050 --> 00:24:38.467
do you think it
would-- what would

00:24:38.467 --> 00:24:41.060
be the phenomenon in an image?

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:41.560
Edges.

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:42.260
What's that?

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:43.590
You get edges.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:46.690
Yeah, you would you
get edges and regions

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:48.820
with a constant grayscale value.

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:49.390
Yeah.

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.348
So, actually, what you'd
end up getting is like

00:24:51.348 --> 00:24:55.420
cartoonish-looking things
because you take an image

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:56.320
with--

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:59.350
8-bit or whatever
it is, 8-bit stuff,

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.900
and then you end up with
three different values,

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.100
and you get regions.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:06.640
So, OK, these are
just sort of examples

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:09.100
to give you the idea of
how these things are done.

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:12.520
But, OK.

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:16.360
Next topic, which is something
we're going to talk about also

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.730
in other contexts, like
a statistical context,

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:20.900
is the idea of
robust approximation.

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:22.630
So I mean, robustness
is this thing--

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:24.610
I think we've talked
about it before.

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:27.150
It's a universal thing.

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:30.530
If you do anything
with math at all,

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:33.010
then it's something you
need to be aware of.

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:36.280
Your model is not
what you think it is.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:38.350
Might be really
accurate if we're

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:43.300
talking electromagnetics or
photonics or E&amp;M or Newtonian

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:44.050
mechanics.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:46.240
It's pretty accurate, thank you.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:48.190
But in other cases,
it's not at all, right?

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:51.430
That would be in areas
like machine learning

00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:54.190
or, for that matter, finance.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:57.760
Your model is just a
fantasy, and it's not

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.740
what's actually happening.

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.320
Newtonian mechanics,
I'd say it's kind of

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:03.760
is what's happening.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.940
E&amp;M, your model is actually
kind of what's happening.

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:09.503
So although even there,
there's questions.

00:26:09.503 --> 00:26:11.420
You might not know stuff
and things like that.

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:13.100
OK.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.960
So here it is.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.450
We want to, let's say-- we
want an approximation problem.

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:20.780
We want to minimize
norm AX minus b.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:24.590
But we are-- we're going
to actually address

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:30.320
the idea that we don't
know A. So don't know A.

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:33.680
By the way, let's talk about
how do most people in practice

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:36.370
handle uncertainty.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:39.850
What's the most common method
for handling uncertainty?

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:40.735
It's not fancy.

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.710
How do most people
handle uncertainty?

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:49.050
I mean, look, right
now as we're speaking,

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:51.600
people are doing supply--
they're working out supply--

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.390
I guarantee you Amazon is
working out supply chains.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.310
They're deciding
stuff that's going

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:59.700
to go to various warehouses
and land at fulfillment centers

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:01.980
in the Bay Area.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:04.710
Obviously, they don't-- so how
do they handle the uncertainty,

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:06.330
mostly?

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:07.830
They have a mean
and some variance,

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.270
assume a probability
distribution.

00:27:09.270 --> 00:27:10.590
That's a good--
so the question is

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:12.340
they have a mean and
a variance for A way.

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.380
Turns out that's not true.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:14.880
OK.

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.370
It's even dumber.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:20.220
What's the simplest way
to handle uncertainty?

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.740
They simulate
multiple scenarios.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:24.458
I mean, you guys are too--

00:27:24.458 --> 00:27:27.000
what do you-- you really think
things are that sophisticated?

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:28.420
What do you do?

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:30.420
Think about a safety
stock, I think.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:31.350
Yeah.

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:32.800
I can't believe this.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:33.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:34.998
It's time for you
to know something

00:27:34.998 --> 00:27:37.740
about the real world,
which is horrifying.

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:39.700
What is the simplest way
to handle uncertainty?

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:40.200
They guess.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:40.830
What?

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:41.460
They guess.

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:42.150
They guess.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:42.650
OK.

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:44.525
Now, what is that-- oh,
you're getting close.

00:27:44.525 --> 00:27:45.700
What does that mean, guess?

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:46.665
Educated guess.

00:27:46.665 --> 00:27:47.290
Educated guess.

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:47.890
OK.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:49.540
So but in particular,
here's what

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:51.100
they do-- they just ignore it.

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:52.000
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:53.260
You just ignore it, right?

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:55.360
I mean that's a--

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:58.990
I guarantee you that's how
99% of actual uncertainty,

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:00.610
in practice, is handled.

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:03.130
You're like-- you're
like, this is the demand

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:06.530
we think for these products
over the next hours and days,

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:07.790
or something like that.

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:11.800
And you're like-- you
just say, cool, thanks.

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:14.080
And then you solve like
some big convex problem

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:15.550
to figure out what to do.

00:28:15.550 --> 00:28:19.160
That's actually kind
of what happens, right?

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:20.338
I mean, not always.

00:28:20.338 --> 00:28:21.130
You just ignore it.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:23.680
By the way, ignoring it
and then just go ahead

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.580
and implementing it
is a very bad idea.

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:28.000
So the least you could
do-- and now I'll

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:31.400
take some of these
suggestions-- is what?

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:34.230
The least you can do, if you
ignore it-- if someone says,

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:37.580
look, here's my data,
and then they say, oh,

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:38.490
the data is really--

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:42.290
it changes quite-- it can be
not what I'm saying, right?

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:43.980
And you say, yeah, no problem.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:44.660
Thanks.

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:47.250
And if they give you--

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:47.750
anyway.

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:50.180
If they give you 50 different
values for-- possible values

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:52.640
for A, you just take the
average or something like that

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:54.140
and then go with it, right?

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:55.415
But what should you do then?

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:01.608
What would you do at that point?

00:29:01.608 --> 00:29:02.900
Well, at the least, you-- yeah?

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:04.790
You can simulate the analysis.

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:08.030
Yeah, you simulate it with that.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.880
So that would be probably the--

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:13.160
I mean, the ones that I
don't think are criminal,

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:16.400
that would be an approach
that's not criminal, right?

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.130
If someone comes
to you and says,

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:21.890
oh, I've generated samples
from what I think A is.

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.990
And you go, cool,
thank you. right?

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:27.230
Then the first thing you do
is you form the average of A

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:29.070
or the median or
something like that.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:30.110
Use it in this thing.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:34.010
You get an x, and then you
simulate this thing across all

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.030
the individual samples.

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:36.630
Everybody following that?

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:40.520
And by the way, if it's pretty
tight, you just say, done,

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:43.340
right?

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:44.280
So that's the least.

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:44.780
OK.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:48.590
So there is a--

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.810
so there are methods for
handling uncertainty.

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:52.880
Like I said, anyone who
does anything in practice

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:54.710
anywhere is going to
have regularization.

00:29:54.710 --> 00:29:56.630
They may not call
it regularization.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:59.060
They might call it robustness.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:00.450
That would be one name for it.

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:01.880
It's got lots of names.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:04.520
But you can't make
anything practical work

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:12.440
without addressing this
uncertainty somehow.

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:15.470
Actually, all of regularization
in machine learning statistics,

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:19.430
all of it is nothing but
addressing uncertainty

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:24.380
like this in data
and stuff like that.

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.600
OK. let's take-- so
we'll look at a couple--

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:31.200
now we're going to look at
fancy methods for doing this.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.698
Well, there's
different approaches.

00:30:32.698 --> 00:30:34.740
I think we saw these in
some very early examples.

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:37.020
But so one is stochastic.

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:39.270
So you say A comes
from a distribution.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:41.010
They come from a
name distribution,

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:42.400
or you could have--

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:42.900
who knows?

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:44.733
It could be-- it could
actually be something

00:30:44.733 --> 00:30:48.450
from which you can sample A's if
you have a fancy model for it,

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:48.960
right?

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:51.270
That would be something.

00:30:51.270 --> 00:30:54.360
Another actually probably
better defensible

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:58.137
is you estimate A. A is itself.

00:30:58.137 --> 00:31:00.345
There's a whole department
that's estimating A. By A,

00:31:00.345 --> 00:31:01.800
I just mean the model, right?

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:03.133
Or people say the forward model.

00:31:03.133 --> 00:31:04.383
The whole department doing it.

00:31:04.383 --> 00:31:05.490
And they could do things.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:09.290
Like, what you do is you say to
them, don't give me one model.

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:12.260
Give me 50 plausible models.

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:15.420
And what they would then
do is they would say, cool,

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:18.260
if you're going with one, here's
the A we think you should use.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.740
But here's 50 others that are
consistent with everything

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:23.337
we know and we have seen.

00:31:23.337 --> 00:31:24.920
Everybody understand
what I just said?

00:31:24.920 --> 00:31:27.500
So that's, like, ideal.

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:29.300
That's, like, OK,
thank you, right?

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.220
Or you could say
here's 1,000 because I

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:35.210
have that department that does
the modeling, says, I actually

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:36.440
have a generative model.

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:39.290
I can generate as many-- how
many A's you want, right?

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:41.360
I'll just sample from
that distribution.

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:43.910
Everybody following this?

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:47.420
So that's stochastic.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:50.720
And then you might do something
like actually minimize

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:51.600
the expected value.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:53.300
Now, that's a convex
problem, I mean,

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:57.080
because expectation
preserves minimization--

00:31:57.080 --> 00:32:01.610
sorry, expectation
preserves convexity, right?

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:04.640
So, OK.

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:07.160
At the other end,
people do worst case.

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:10.850
And this would be
something like, look,

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:11.955
I don't even believe it.

00:32:11.955 --> 00:32:14.330
It's not generated from a
probability distribution, which

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:15.500
is true of almost everything.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:16.792
Like, almost nothing is, right?

00:32:16.792 --> 00:32:18.260
So it's ridiculous.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:21.200
It's not from a
probability distribution.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:24.140
You'd say, but my
experience says

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:26.420
I think that the
entries in this matrix

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:29.687
are correct plus/minus 3%.

00:32:29.687 --> 00:32:32.270
I mean, it could be a lot more
sophisticated than that, right?

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.020
You could say, well,
the diagonal entries,

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:34.790
I know this well.

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:36.530
These things-- oh, that entry?

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:38.300
I barely know the sign.

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:39.502
Everybody following this?

00:32:39.502 --> 00:32:40.710
It would be things like that.

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:42.630
So you could make up
something like that.

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:44.463
And then you could say,
well, which A is it?

00:32:44.463 --> 00:32:46.370
And you'd say, any
A that satisfies

00:32:46.370 --> 00:32:51.230
the constraints that I just
described to you is plausible.

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:52.670
And then by the
way, people have--

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:54.660
they have huge
philosophical fights,

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:55.970
which is kind of hilarious.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:59.720
So the worst-case, people
say, like, oh, my god, it's

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:03.020
so unsophisticated to
assume a distribution.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.860
And they're like,
that's ridiculous

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:06.960
because it's not a probability
distribution, blah, blah, blah,

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:07.793
and stuff like that.

00:33:07.793 --> 00:33:10.530
And then I don't know,
the stochastic people--

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:13.140
it'd be like a fight between
Bayesians and frequentists

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:13.900
or whatever.

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:14.400
Anyway.

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:18.403
So, and then the stochastic one
would say something like, oh,

00:33:18.403 --> 00:33:20.070
the worst-case is way
too conservative--

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:21.030
and you're like--

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:23.130
none of this makes
any sense, by the way.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:26.283
They're both-- but they're
much closer than they think.

00:33:26.283 --> 00:33:28.200
And actually, a lot of
times it's embarrassing

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:30.540
because they have this huge
philosophical argument,

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.310
and then it turns
out you work out

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.102
the math of what they're
doing, and they're

00:33:34.102 --> 00:33:37.680
doing exactly the
same thing, where

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:41.620
one person has a knob that
says how big their uncertainty

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:42.120
set is.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.120
And the other has a knob
that's basically, like,

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:45.720
variance or something like that.

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:48.630
So it turns out that they;re
kind of the same thing.

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:50.880
Oh, and there are
hybrids of these.

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:55.230
There's robust stochastic.

00:33:55.230 --> 00:34:00.960
And then you'd say, well,
here's my uncertain parameter.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:02.490
From a probability distribution.

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:04.782
And you go, cool, do you mind
giving me the parameters?

00:34:04.782 --> 00:34:06.420
And you go, aha, no.

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:08.310
I gave you a set of
parameters, right?

00:34:08.310 --> 00:34:12.617
And then they would claim that
this gives you the advantages

00:34:12.617 --> 00:34:13.909
of both of these methods or so.

00:34:13.909 --> 00:34:19.380
Anyway, this is just to give you
the big picture of uncertainty

00:34:19.380 --> 00:34:20.969
modeling and things like that.

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:22.139
OK.

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:24.219
All right.

00:34:24.219 --> 00:34:24.719
OK.

00:34:24.719 --> 00:34:28.560
So here's just a baby example
to see what the idea is.

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:30.405
So here-- I mean,
this is a matrix.

00:34:30.405 --> 00:34:31.530
I don't know how big it is.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:33.570
It doesn't really matter,
but it's a matrix, right?

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:35.028
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:34:35.028 --> 00:34:37.320
going to have extremely
explicit uncertainty.

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:38.550
It's extremely dumb.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:42.030
There's a line segment in--

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:44.190
there's a line segment
in matrix space.

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:47.040
I mean, that's kind of silly
because it's-- a more common

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:49.983
model would be all the-- these
entries are all plus/minus 1%

00:34:49.983 --> 00:34:51.150
and things like that, right?

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:53.010
So, OK.

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.672
But this is just
to make the example

00:34:54.672 --> 00:34:56.380
so we can work out
exactly what happened.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:35:00.790
So the A that you get
is parameterized by u.

00:35:00.790 --> 00:35:01.290
OK.

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:02.680
Here it is.

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:05.490
And so this is what
people would normally do.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:07.680
They would say, what
does my matrix look like?

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:09.600
It looks like that. u
is centered around 0,

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:10.680
and they'd say cool.

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:14.550
My A matrix is
A0, period, right?

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:18.490
And that would give
you this curve here.

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:21.210
And what this is
showing you is here,

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:23.520
this is what happens
when is A is A0.

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:26.070
This is what happens
as you change A.

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:28.437
And you can see,
well, this is going

00:35:28.437 --> 00:35:29.520
to be these other methods.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:30.853
They're not as good as this one.

00:35:30.853 --> 00:35:33.030
This one has the absolute
minimum value here.

00:35:33.030 --> 00:35:35.835
And you can see that
as your errors-- like,

00:35:35.835 --> 00:35:36.960
I don't know what this is--

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:39.480
3 and then by the time this
gets to minus 1, it's double.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:40.650
It's 6.

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:43.020
And so you might say, well,
this is too sensitive.

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:49.070
Then, you might look
at this stochastic one.

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:51.260
So here's this-- this
is the stochastic one.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:56.210
And what we do there is we
minimize basically the integral

00:35:56.210 --> 00:36:00.840
of this over this range, and
that gives you this one here.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.320
And what you can see
here is you-- so here's

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:05.900
how people would say
this-- you have given up

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:08.660
nominal performance.

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:10.520
So you gave up
nominal performance.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:14.390
And what you got was
something that was much less

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:16.460
sensitive to the variations.

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:17.720
Everybody see this?

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:20.130
I mean, that's kind of
the story you want that.

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.760
That is exactly the story you--
that's you want to happen,

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:23.330
right?

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:26.420
And then the worst-case
one, again, you

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.610
can-- that's not hard to solve.

00:36:28.610 --> 00:36:32.060
And it turns out for
the worst-case one here,

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.420
you could see you gave
up some performance here,

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:38.390
but you can see that
it's incredibly robust.

00:36:38.390 --> 00:36:39.840
So everybody got--

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:42.110
So this picture plays
out and obviously

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:44.130
in much more
sophisticated settings,

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:46.050
but this is the
story that you want

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:49.890
to have when you have a method
that addresses robustness

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:50.920
or uncertainty.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:54.340
So all this make sense?

00:36:54.340 --> 00:36:54.840
Yeah.

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:57.975
So I can tell you
a little trick.

00:36:57.975 --> 00:36:59.820
Do you want to hear a--

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:02.280
yeah, but this is unofficial,
so you can't tell people

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:04.060
where you learned this.

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:04.560
OK.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:06.450
So it turns out--

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:11.220
as a street fighting matter,
it turns out that all you need

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:16.440
is just literally a shockingly
small number of samples of A.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:19.780
And then you do something like a
minimax or something like that,

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:21.820
which would be
something like this--

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:25.440
a very crude approximation
of this worst-case thing

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:26.790
does the trick.

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:31.050
So for example, if you
say, design me a trajectory

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:33.810
for, I don't know, this
drone or something--

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:35.250
goes from here to there.

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:37.260
And you go, like, OK,
can I have the model?

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:38.385
And they go, yeah, sure.

00:37:38.385 --> 00:37:40.260
And they go, well, what
are these parameters?

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:41.760
And you go, we don't know.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:42.660
They can vary.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:44.935
That's the-- I mean, because
it depends on the payload

00:37:44.935 --> 00:37:45.810
and blah, blah, blah.

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:48.690
And by the way, even if
I tell you the payload,

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:51.990
I might not know it within
plus or minus 5% or something

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:53.470
like that.

00:37:53.470 --> 00:37:56.120
So there, it turns out
an extremely good thing

00:37:56.120 --> 00:37:58.620
to do-- is you just ask whoever
is coming up with the model,

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:01.650
you'd say, give me 10 models.

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:03.210
And you say, here
are the rules--

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:04.650
they have to be plausible.

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:08.640
They can't be-- they can't
give you some crazy thing.

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.530
You'd say, every
one of these you

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:12.533
would have to defend
as a plausible model.

00:38:12.533 --> 00:38:13.950
That it's consistent
with what you

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:15.900
know about the problem
and the past data.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:16.890
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:18.630
And they could do that
all sorts of-- so you

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:19.290
might do it this way.

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:20.430
You'd say, not a problem.

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:23.700
I'll fit a model on Monday data,
Tuesday data, Wednesday data,

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:24.450
and Thursday data.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:24.990
Done.

00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:26.760
Now, you have seven
different models.

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:29.990
If they're wildly different,
something's weird.

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:31.773
If they're all the
same as doubles,

00:38:31.773 --> 00:38:33.065
what do you think has happened?

00:38:36.507 --> 00:38:38.090
What would you do
if you asked someone

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:40.243
to make a model of
something and they go, cool,

00:38:40.243 --> 00:38:42.410
I made one for Monday,
Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday?

00:38:42.410 --> 00:38:45.180
And you go, god, thanks so much.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:48.120
And then they're kind of
like equal as doubles.

00:38:51.140 --> 00:38:55.550
It's not a convex
optimization question here.

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:56.660
What would you do?

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:02.950
Variation is not dependent
on the day, so it's--

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:05.620
Nah, nah, that's not-- no.

00:39:05.620 --> 00:39:06.370
Yeah.

00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:08.980
Really, you think out of the
eighth significant figure,

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:09.670
it doesn't--

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:10.600
no.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:12.590
It's fraud.

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:13.900
That's what it is.

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:15.070
They just lied.

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:17.260
They just said
they just gave you

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:20.110
seven copies of a single model.

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:22.152
So the least they should
do is cover their tracks

00:39:22.152 --> 00:39:24.402
and add a little bit of
[? rand/and ?] into the model.

00:39:24.402 --> 00:39:25.510
I mean, come on, right?

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:27.700
So if you're in this situation
and some crazy person

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:29.830
comes to you and say
get 10 models, yeah.

00:39:29.830 --> 00:39:33.470
So just for fun,
that'd be one extreme.

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:38.843
And so fraud is one approach
to generating a set of models.

00:39:38.843 --> 00:39:40.510
Let's do a principled
one in statistics.

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:42.790
We're jumping ahead, but
I don't mind doing it.

00:39:42.790 --> 00:39:46.033
How would you make a
principled one in statistics?

00:39:46.033 --> 00:39:47.950
Fitting a model, let's
say maximum likelihood.

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:50.320
Sorry, I'm getting ahead,
but you can just pretend.

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:52.028
If you don't know what
I'm talking about,

00:39:52.028 --> 00:39:53.380
that's fine, too.

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:58.970
Someone told me a good way
to honor a request for 10

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:00.500
plausible models.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:02.780
We'll do maximum
likelihood here.

00:40:02.780 --> 00:40:05.555
Someone tell me how
to get 10 models.

00:40:05.555 --> 00:40:06.680
That's what I want to know.

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.500
And don't pretend there's no
one in statistics here because I

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:12.230
know there's a lot of people
in statistics here, so don't--

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:14.210
yeah, go ahead.

00:40:14.210 --> 00:40:16.340
Somebody make a suggestion.

00:40:16.340 --> 00:40:18.350
You were going to make one?

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:20.450
Do you bootstrap just like 10--

00:40:20.450 --> 00:40:21.410
That would be fine.

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:22.610
Bootstrap would be fine.

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:24.890
Here's a simple way that
is convex optimization.

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:25.910
You would do this--

00:40:25.910 --> 00:40:29.480
certainly, you would find the
maximum likelihood estimate.

00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:31.970
And you'd send that parameter
and say, if you have

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:34.170
to go with one, take this one.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:34.670
OK.

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:36.230
But then what you
would do is you'd

00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:37.850
find the maximum likelihood.

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:39.890
I will draw the likelihood
function right now.

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:42.140
Sorry, I'm getting ahead of
myself, but it's just fun.

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:43.800
So here's the
likelihood function.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:46.700
Here's the maximum
likelihood point.

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.230
What you'd do is
you would record

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:50.030
what the maximum likelihood is.

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:53.420
Drop something to
a likelihood level

00:40:53.420 --> 00:40:56.300
that is slightly less than
the maximum likelihood, which

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:58.430
is completely defensible
statistically.

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:04.070
And then what you would do is
you would find points in here

00:41:04.070 --> 00:41:05.060
like that.

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:06.440
I mean, I guess it depends.

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:08.660
If it's all smooth, this
would be like from--

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:12.890
you'd sample from the
fisher information,

00:41:12.890 --> 00:41:14.240
the ellipsoid defined by it.

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.470
OK, sorry, that was weird.

00:41:15.470 --> 00:41:16.637
I just couldn't help myself.

00:41:16.637 --> 00:41:17.762
But did everybody get that?

00:41:17.762 --> 00:41:19.679
And then someone said,
what have you given me?

00:41:19.679 --> 00:41:21.350
And you'd say, I
gave you 10 models.

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:23.210
And you go, which-- well, you
say the first one is actually

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:24.020
the maximum likelihood.

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.353
If you're going to go with
one, I'd go with that of our.

00:41:26.353 --> 00:41:28.550
And you go, what are
the other 10 or 100?

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:33.890
And you would say, oh, those
are models whose parameters

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:38.000
are almost maximum likelihood.

00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:39.170
Everybody got that?

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:39.710
OK.

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:41.670
And then you'd say, thank you.

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:45.300
And then you'd come over here,
and you'd do something with--

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:48.890
and then you'd put these in
as a maximum or something

00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:49.970
like that or an average.

00:41:49.970 --> 00:41:51.140
It wouldn't matter.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:54.230
Oh, sorry, I was going to
tell you the secret on this.

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:58.100
The secret is by simply
admitting and acknowledging

00:41:58.100 --> 00:42:01.570
that there is uncertainty
gets you, like, 90% of the way

00:42:01.570 --> 00:42:02.320
there.

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:05.110
Then you can do all these fancy
things later and, you know,

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:05.840
blah, blah, blah.

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:08.930
But the point is just
doing 10 is good enough.

00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:11.530
So now you know a secret, right?

00:42:11.530 --> 00:42:14.110
So if I go to my forecasting
group and I tell you,

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:17.080
here's the demand for this
product over the next week,

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:19.740
hour by hour, and you go, OK.

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:20.930
You go, are you certain?

00:42:20.930 --> 00:42:22.168
And they're like, no.

00:42:22.168 --> 00:42:23.710
And then they say,
please generate me

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:25.360
10 such trajectories or 100.

00:42:25.360 --> 00:42:26.560
I don't care, right?

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:28.187
Then this tells you it's fine.

00:42:28.187 --> 00:42:30.520
And you say, but the rule is
they have to be defensible.

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:32.830
They can't be, like,
weird things, right?

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:35.320
Every single one would
have to pass as a model.

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:36.550
So that's what you would do.

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:41.100
OK.

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:45.510
So, sorry, that was just a
weird aside on all these things.

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:46.170
OK.

00:42:46.170 --> 00:42:48.540
But this is the picture.

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:53.250
So you can actually do things
like stochastic robust least

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:54.733
squares.

00:42:54.733 --> 00:42:57.150
And I'll go through this because
it actually is very cool,

00:42:57.150 --> 00:42:59.640
and it tells you about what
regularization really is.

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:03.000
So here it is.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:04.690
So we're going to
do least squares.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:05.190
Here we are.

00:43:05.190 --> 00:43:08.950
So it's 1805 now, and
we're doing least squares.

00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:11.820
And so our model is
going to be stochastic.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:15.240
We're going to say, well, no,
I don't know A. A is A bar.

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:18.840
That's kind of like
a nominal value of A,

00:43:18.840 --> 00:43:21.990
plus U. Use a random
matrix, 0 mean.

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:25.740
And let's say that the expected
value [INAUDIBLE] is p.

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:28.560
So if p were like
little sigma squared I,

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:32.160
it says the entries are
all completely independent,

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:34.860
and they're all perturbed
by an n0 sigma squared,

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:37.900
little sigma squared
value independently,

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:39.630
and completely reasonable.

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:41.440
By the way, if someone
gives you a matrix

00:43:41.440 --> 00:43:43.148
and you say how accurate
are the entries?

00:43:43.148 --> 00:43:46.150
And they can't say,
find someone else

00:43:46.150 --> 00:43:49.020
to work with because
in any practical--

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:53.040
if you can't say something
like, oh, those are pretty good,

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:58.032
like, third significant digit
is actually probably right.

00:43:58.032 --> 00:43:58.740
That's one thing.

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:02.190
Or if they say the second
digit is meaningless.

00:44:02.190 --> 00:44:05.250
So if they can't say anything,
then just find someone else

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:07.020
to work with because
this is not someone

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:08.228
who knows what they're doing.

00:44:08.228 --> 00:44:09.630
Everybody got that?

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:10.442
OK.

00:44:10.442 --> 00:44:12.900
All right, so this is one of
those cases where you can just

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:14.310
analytically work this out.

00:44:14.310 --> 00:44:16.620
I mean, you just expand
this, and you get

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:18.360
something really, really cool.

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:22.230
It turns out you should
solve this problem here.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:25.320
It's super interesting--
it's super simple, right?

00:44:25.320 --> 00:44:31.050
And actually, now, let's
just take p equals delta I.

00:44:31.050 --> 00:44:35.010
So delta would be little sigma
squared I. Then it's very cool.

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:35.932
You end up with this.

00:44:35.932 --> 00:44:37.890
And you go, oh, that's
Tikhonov regularization.

00:44:37.890 --> 00:44:39.828
So you can turn this around.

00:44:39.828 --> 00:44:41.370
And someone says,
what are you doing?

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:44.250
And you go, well, I'm doing
regularized least squares.

00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:46.560
I'm doing ridge regression,
you would say here.

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:48.760
Or you'd say Tikhonov
regularization or something

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:49.600
like that.

00:44:49.600 --> 00:44:51.743
And they'd say, why?

00:44:51.743 --> 00:44:53.410
And one reason-- one
thing you could say

00:44:53.410 --> 00:44:56.407
is, yeah, because I
don't know A, right?

00:44:56.407 --> 00:44:58.240
And I think we already
talked about the fact

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:01.630
that this makes sense because
the thing that happened--

00:45:01.630 --> 00:45:06.685
Ax is sort of the result. If
A is uncertain and x is big,

00:45:06.685 --> 00:45:08.740
you get a lot of
uncertainty in the result.

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:11.500
If x is small, like if it's 0,
it doesn't matter what A is.

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:13.060
Everybody following this?

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:15.880
So this all sort of makes
sense, but it's very cool so.

00:45:15.880 --> 00:45:18.250
It means that,
well, now you know

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:19.930
what taking Tikhonov
regularization is

00:45:19.930 --> 00:45:21.130
or ridge regression is.

00:45:21.130 --> 00:45:23.110
It's basically saying
that all of your data--

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:28.660
I guess in, let's see,
in a regression context,

00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:31.930
you're basically saying, thank
you for all your features,

00:45:31.930 --> 00:45:35.260
your feature matrix
because that's what A is.

00:45:35.260 --> 00:45:38.050
Basically, it says I don't
trust all these features are

00:45:38.050 --> 00:45:41.290
plus/minus 0.01 with--

00:45:41.290 --> 00:45:43.510
they're disturbed by
independent Gaussians.

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:44.565
Everybody following this?

00:45:44.565 --> 00:45:45.940
So that's what
this-- and then it

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:49.870
says, that suggests a
Tikhonov parameter, right?

00:45:49.870 --> 00:45:53.577
So that makes sense?

00:45:53.577 --> 00:45:54.660
I think it's kind of cool.

00:45:57.197 --> 00:45:59.030
I'm not going to go
into the details of this

00:45:59.030 --> 00:46:01.250
because it's actually
quite complicated.

00:46:01.250 --> 00:46:04.230
But you can actually
do a weird thing with--

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:06.230
you can actually
have-- you can actually

00:46:06.230 --> 00:46:10.040
work out the case
where A is in-- this is

00:46:10.040 --> 00:46:11.960
an ellipsoid in matrix space.

00:46:11.960 --> 00:46:13.985
That's what that is.

00:46:13.985 --> 00:46:15.860
This would be the analog
of the previous one.

00:46:15.860 --> 00:46:18.045
In the previous one,
I made a-- well,

00:46:18.045 --> 00:46:19.670
I didn't say what
the distribution was,

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:21.980
but I told you its first
and second moments.

00:46:21.980 --> 00:46:24.590
But I didn't say it was Gaussian
or anything, but anyway.

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:29.660
So here this would be like an--
this would be the ellipsoid.

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:31.820
And you'd say, any
matrix in this ellipsoid

00:46:31.820 --> 00:46:33.887
is a plausible model.

00:46:33.887 --> 00:46:35.970
You can actually-- I'm not
going to go through it,

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:38.480
but it's actually-- these are
things that, oh, let's say,

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.610
Lagrange would not have known.

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:42.335
Let's just put it that way.

00:46:42.335 --> 00:46:43.960
Probably could have
explained it to him

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:45.168
pretty quickly, to be honest.

00:46:45.168 --> 00:46:47.650
But nevertheless-- well, OK,
they did have some questions,

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:50.140
like, how on earth do
you solve that problem?

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:53.260
But we'd have an
answer for that, too?

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:56.530
So, OK, I'm not
going to look at it.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:59.500
I won't go into the details
because it's quite complicated,

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:00.958
but I'll just show
you the results,

00:47:00.958 --> 00:47:02.833
which are really very cool.

00:47:02.833 --> 00:47:04.750
Oh, and this is, by the
way, the kind of thing

00:47:04.750 --> 00:47:07.840
you'd expect to have.

00:47:07.840 --> 00:47:11.440
Let me explain what we're doing.

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:14.410
So, here's what we're
doing is, this is now--

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:16.090
I have a disk in matrix space.

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:18.070
I mean, it's a baby
example, but I have a disk.

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:19.900
That's what this is
if you think about it.

00:47:19.900 --> 00:47:22.010
It's two-dimensional.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:24.310
It goes off in these two
directions, A1 and A2.

00:47:24.310 --> 00:47:25.030
I have a disk.

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:28.750
And actually, I put a uniform
distribution on the disk,

00:47:28.750 --> 00:47:30.100
on the unit--

00:47:30.100 --> 00:47:31.600
this unit disk.

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:33.110
That's what actually happens.

00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:35.050
So this one is cool.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:36.280
This is x least squares.

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:38.230
That just says that's
the traditional method

00:47:38.230 --> 00:47:40.420
of handling uncertainty,
which is ignore it.

00:47:40.420 --> 00:47:44.600
And I just plug in the
mean and I calculate x.

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:46.460
And then you get
this distribution

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:48.920
like this, which you should
do anyway just to check.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:53.637
And that's a message to you that
you probably shouldn't-- that

00:47:53.637 --> 00:47:55.970
you need to be a little bit
more sophisticated than just

00:47:55.970 --> 00:47:58.850
ignoring uncertainty.

00:47:58.850 --> 00:48:00.800
And let me just check
on a few things here.

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:01.970
Let's describe.

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:04.805
How would you describe
in words these events?

00:48:07.700 --> 00:48:08.690
What would you say?

00:48:13.560 --> 00:48:14.740
Worst-case scenario?

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:15.240
Yeah.

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:16.573
You just say, look, this is bad.

00:48:16.573 --> 00:48:18.930
These are perturbations
to A where my choice of x

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:21.450
gave me a really big residual.

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:24.420
So this is bad luck or
something Oh, by the way,

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:27.420
how do you describe these?

00:48:27.420 --> 00:48:28.970
Best-case good luck.

00:48:28.970 --> 00:48:29.720
Yeah.

00:48:29.720 --> 00:48:30.980
What'd you call them?

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:31.520
Good luck.

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:32.600
Yeah.

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:34.340
How about dumb luck?

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:36.500
Because you're using
a dumb method, right?

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:39.260
And sure enough, 5% of the
time, the perturbation of A

00:48:39.260 --> 00:48:42.170
is in a way that
actually helped you.

00:48:42.170 --> 00:48:44.105
So this is-- I would
call this dumb luck.

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:51.290
Then we look at Tikhonov
here, or ridge regression,

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:53.990
if you want to-- that's a
different dialect for it.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:56.120
So if you do ridge
regression, you get this.

00:48:56.120 --> 00:48:58.280
And actually, it's
really cool to see what--

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:02.210
I mean, you see immediately kind
of what adding regularization

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:03.050
does.

00:49:03.050 --> 00:49:05.760
It tightens the distribution
with respect-- in this case,

00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:08.600
it's with respect to a
very specific perturbation

00:49:08.600 --> 00:49:09.830
of the parameters.

00:49:09.830 --> 00:49:10.790
So that's this one.

00:49:10.790 --> 00:49:15.350
And then when you do the
robustly squares, I mean,

00:49:15.350 --> 00:49:19.670
that's unfair because we told
it exactly what the disk was

00:49:19.670 --> 00:49:20.580
and stuff like that.

00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:22.413
And you can see you get
this beautiful, very

00:49:22.413 --> 00:49:23.630
tight distribution.

00:49:23.630 --> 00:49:26.880
So make sense?

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:27.420
Yeah.

00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:29.260
OK.

00:49:29.260 --> 00:49:29.760
OK.

00:49:29.760 --> 00:49:34.040
So this is-- and these are
all convex problems and stuff

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:34.540
like that.

00:49:34.540 --> 00:49:37.320
So, OK.

00:49:37.320 --> 00:49:42.420
All right, so our next topic
is statistical estimation.

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:46.770
It's extremely elementary,
but we'll talk about--

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:47.858
it's sort of interesting.

00:49:47.858 --> 00:49:49.650
So actually, it's one
of those things where

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:52.817
if you haven't seen this stuff
before, you are probably--

00:49:52.817 --> 00:49:54.900
you're not going to
understand it from what I say.

00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:56.130
And if you've seen
it before, you'll

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:58.172
be bored out of your mind
or something like that.

00:49:58.172 --> 00:50:00.960
So I'll probably tell some weird
stories through it, anyway,

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:04.150
just to make it more
interesting, but here we go.

00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:07.020
So we'll just start with
parametric distribution

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:07.830
estimation.

00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:14.580
So here's the idea, is
that we have a density--

00:50:14.580 --> 00:50:17.580
and we have an observed
value of something.

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:20.550
We have a density,
but the density

00:50:20.550 --> 00:50:21.990
is indexed by a parameter.

00:50:21.990 --> 00:50:24.390
For example, a Gaussian
would be indexed

00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:28.260
by, let's say, its mean and its
covariance matrix, let's say,

00:50:28.260 --> 00:50:28.770
right?

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:33.690
Or a Poisson variable by its
rate or something like this,

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:36.730
or an exponential variable by
its rate or whatever it is.

00:50:36.730 --> 00:50:39.370
So you'd have parameters.

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:43.660
And what we'll do is we'll put
the parameter as a subscript.

00:50:43.660 --> 00:50:50.050
So Px of y is going to be the
density with parameter values

00:50:50.050 --> 00:50:54.070
x at the point y.

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:56.470
So that's going to be--

00:50:56.470 --> 00:50:57.910
that is going to be--

00:50:57.910 --> 00:51:02.710
that that's called the
likelihood function.

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:06.520
People often work with the
log likelihood function.

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:08.980
Obviously, maximizing a
likelihood and a log likelihood

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:09.890
are the same thing.

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:12.320
And so you would have
something that looks like this.

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:15.070
So here this is an
estimator for x,

00:51:15.070 --> 00:51:17.590
and you're given y,
which is an observation.

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:19.445
And I mean, it kind
of makes sense,

00:51:19.445 --> 00:51:21.320
and you could argue a
lot of things about it.

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:23.770
And if you take a
statistics 101 class,

00:51:23.770 --> 00:51:27.080
you'll hear a lot about
properties of this.

00:51:27.080 --> 00:51:27.580
OK.

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:31.460
So that's maximum
likelihood estimation.

00:51:31.460 --> 00:51:33.975
So as I said, this is called
the log likelihood function.

00:51:33.975 --> 00:51:35.350
By the way, here
what we're doing

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:38.380
is we're considering
this a function of x,

00:51:38.380 --> 00:51:41.070
which is the parameter in
the distribution, not of y.

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:49.210
And you can add
constraints to this.

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:52.660
I mean, for example, if x
is-- if this is a Gaussian

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:57.910
distribution, let's say, scalar
with mean mu and standard

00:51:57.910 --> 00:52:02.470
deviation little sigma,
then little sigma

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:03.520
has to be positive.

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:05.390
Like, otherwise, it
makes no sense at all.

00:52:05.390 --> 00:52:07.930
So you could add-- you would
add that as a constraint,

00:52:07.930 --> 00:52:11.590
or simply define the
likelihood to be 0.

00:52:11.590 --> 00:52:17.890
If you say, well, what
density do I get when--

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:21.430
what's my likelihood if I guess
that the standard deviation is

00:52:21.430 --> 00:52:22.420
minus 1?

00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:24.550
I mean, aside from that
being profoundly stupid,

00:52:24.550 --> 00:52:26.230
you just get 0.

00:52:26.230 --> 00:52:29.900
And then log of 0
is minus infinity.

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:32.440
And that means you did a
very bad job of maximizing.

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:34.390
So, OK.

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:36.040
You can add regularization, too.

00:52:36.040 --> 00:52:40.160
So, regularized maximum
likelihood you could do.

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:41.980
So the question is,
when is this convex?

00:52:41.980 --> 00:52:49.070
And it's convex when the log
likelihood function is concave.

00:52:49.070 --> 00:52:52.250
So now you have to be super
careful because up till now, we

00:52:52.250 --> 00:52:56.370
saw the idea of a log concave
distribution or density.

00:52:56.370 --> 00:53:00.830
So a density is log
concave if, when you fix x,

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:03.380
this thing is concave and y.

00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:06.230
That's a log concave density.

00:53:06.230 --> 00:53:08.900
This is not that.

00:53:08.900 --> 00:53:10.410
This is the opposite.

00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:14.960
This is, how does this
vary as a function of when

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:18.890
you fix y of x, which is the
parameter in the distribution?

00:53:18.890 --> 00:53:19.730
Everybody got that?

00:53:19.730 --> 00:53:22.490
Now, it's going to turn out
that there's lots of cases--

00:53:22.490 --> 00:53:24.560
like, if it's an
exponential family,

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:27.750
I mean, then it's going to turn
out they're the same thing.

00:53:27.750 --> 00:53:30.242
But in general,
they're different.

00:53:30.242 --> 00:53:31.700
So I'm just going
slow just to make

00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:34.300
sure this is all totally clear.

00:53:34.300 --> 00:53:34.800
OK.

00:53:34.800 --> 00:53:35.760
So that's it.

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:40.320
So roughly speaking, maximum
likelihood is estimation

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:46.600
is a convex problem when the log
likelihood function is concave,

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:51.630
which is not the same as saying
that the densities are all log

00:53:51.630 --> 00:53:52.150
concave.

00:53:52.150 --> 00:53:54.490
That's a different thing.

00:53:54.490 --> 00:53:54.990
OK.

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:57.340
So we'll just look at
some simple examples here.

00:53:57.340 --> 00:53:59.280
So here's one.

00:53:59.280 --> 00:54:01.950
It's a traditional
linear measurement model.

00:54:01.950 --> 00:54:05.340
So I make a bunch
of measurements,

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:07.260
and they're going
to look like Yi.

00:54:07.260 --> 00:54:11.370
So Yi is-- and my model is
going to be that each Yi,

00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:17.490
which is a scalar, is a
linear function of x here plus

00:54:17.490 --> 00:54:20.020
a noise, which is independent--

00:54:20.020 --> 00:54:22.980
which is independent
across different i's.

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:24.745
So that's what the model is.

00:54:24.745 --> 00:54:27.120
And so actually, it's kind of
interesting because someone

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:29.880
in like EE or something
like that would describe

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:31.200
this in a pretty simple way.

00:54:31.200 --> 00:54:33.600
They would say something
like, x is what you really

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:35.190
want to measure.

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:38.070
Ai tells you what your
i'th measurement is.

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:41.760
Oh, that's a radar snapshot
from this position like that.

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:46.260
Or A7 is what I deduce
from some GPS thing

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:48.210
or something like that.

00:54:48.210 --> 00:54:50.670
And they say, well,
you-- that's how they

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.410
would describe the problem.

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:54.640
But a statistician would
have to describe it

00:54:54.640 --> 00:54:57.490
this way-- they
would say that what

00:54:57.490 --> 00:55:00.250
you observe from your
sensor suite actually

00:55:00.250 --> 00:55:01.480
comes from a distribution.

00:55:01.480 --> 00:55:03.820
And you go, oh, OK.

00:55:03.820 --> 00:55:07.360
And then you say, ah,
the distribution changes.

00:55:07.360 --> 00:55:10.300
It's parametrized by this x.

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:11.320
Everybody got that?

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:12.340
I mean, they're
saying the same thing,

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:14.800
but I'm just saying this is a
language a statistician would

00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:15.300
use.

00:55:15.300 --> 00:55:17.200
And then they would
say, so your problem

00:55:17.200 --> 00:55:20.050
is really to estimate
which distribution

00:55:20.050 --> 00:55:23.660
generated the measurements, the
27 measurements you actually

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:24.160
got.

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.530
Everybody following this?

00:55:26.530 --> 00:55:29.530
Whereas, I think a normal person
would just say something like,

00:55:29.530 --> 00:55:30.610
that's the model.

00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:32.410
These are the 27
measurements I got.

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:33.550
These are independent.

00:55:33.550 --> 00:55:35.500
Could you please
guess what x is?

00:55:35.500 --> 00:55:37.990
So I mean, I have lots of
friends who are statisticians,

00:55:37.990 --> 00:55:38.720
so this is fine.

00:55:38.720 --> 00:55:40.540
So all fine.

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:41.950
Actually, that's how I can--

00:55:41.950 --> 00:55:44.860
I've learned-- I've
picked up their dialect.

00:55:44.860 --> 00:55:48.830
Actually, makes me very nervous
when I-- anyway, so it's--

00:55:48.830 --> 00:55:50.270
which, I, of course, enjoy.

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:50.930
So, anyway.

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:52.020
All right.

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:52.520
OK.

00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:55.160
So let's see how this works.

00:55:55.160 --> 00:56:00.740
Well, if Y-- these
are independent.

00:56:00.740 --> 00:56:03.860
So what you're really saying
here is something like,

00:56:03.860 --> 00:56:06.230
Yi minus Ai transpose
x, which is Vi--

00:56:06.230 --> 00:56:07.940
these are independent.

00:56:07.940 --> 00:56:11.240
So the density is the
product of the densities.

00:56:11.240 --> 00:56:14.270
So the density of the
vector of measurements

00:56:14.270 --> 00:56:17.180
is equal to this
thing over here.

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:21.800
And you take the log of
that, and you get this.

00:56:21.800 --> 00:56:25.460
And here, this is-- we want to--

00:56:25.460 --> 00:56:26.960
the variable here is x.

00:56:26.960 --> 00:56:33.800
So what's interesting
here is this is when is--

00:56:33.800 --> 00:56:36.977
this thing is a
convex problem, which

00:56:36.977 --> 00:56:38.810
is to say that the log
likelihood is convex.

00:56:38.810 --> 00:56:42.080
In this case, it
coincides exactly

00:56:42.080 --> 00:56:44.000
with the density
being log concave.

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:49.650
So this is a case where the two
are the same, because actually

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:51.270
you see something here.

00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:56.700
It's because basically the x--

00:56:56.700 --> 00:56:57.870
if this thing is log--

00:56:57.870 --> 00:57:01.290
so if it's a log concave
density, then this is--

00:57:01.290 --> 00:57:04.320
that tells you that
this is concave in--

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:06.450
log p is concave in this.

00:57:06.450 --> 00:57:10.050
But if log concave
is- if p is such

00:57:10.050 --> 00:57:12.300
that this is a concave
function, then this

00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:15.300
is precomposition with
an affine function.

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:18.330
And so these are
concave functions of x.

00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:19.860
That makes sense?

00:57:19.860 --> 00:57:21.060
So that's the picture.

00:57:21.060 --> 00:57:21.570
OK.

00:57:21.570 --> 00:57:24.000
So all right.

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:26.640
And let's look at some
specific examples.

00:57:26.640 --> 00:57:29.370
So someone says, OK,
the noises are Gaussian.

00:57:29.370 --> 00:57:32.970
And you'd say, well, here's
the log likelihood function.

00:57:32.970 --> 00:57:38.280
And you see it's a constant
minus, a quadratic.

00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:41.940
And what you see immediately is
the maximum likelihood solution

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:44.300
is the least squares solution.

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:46.050
So this says if you're
doing least squares

00:57:46.050 --> 00:57:48.780
and you want to make it sound
fancy and brush someone off,

00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:50.072
they'd say, what are you doing?

00:57:50.072 --> 00:57:53.280
And you go, oh, I'm doing
maximum likelihood estimation

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:55.560
with a Gaussian distribution,
noise distribution,

00:57:55.560 --> 00:57:57.090
or something like that.

00:57:57.090 --> 00:58:00.870
So everybody got that?

00:58:00.870 --> 00:58:02.700
OK.

00:58:02.700 --> 00:58:05.400
You could have--
let's make the noise--

00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:07.650
let's make the noise Laplacian.

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:12.300
So Laplacian means that it
looks like-- the density looks

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:15.330
like this.

00:58:15.330 --> 00:58:17.130
Here's 0.

00:58:17.130 --> 00:58:20.070
And it goes like this.

00:58:20.070 --> 00:58:25.560
And out here, it's got way, way
bigger tails than a Gaussian

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:30.330
and for the same-- let's say,
for the same whatever variance.

00:58:30.330 --> 00:58:35.118
So that's-- and what this says
is you now take the log of this

00:58:35.118 --> 00:58:36.160
and you end up with this.

00:58:36.160 --> 00:58:37.650
And you see that's cool.

00:58:37.650 --> 00:58:44.070
So maximum likelihood
estimation with Laplacian noise

00:58:44.070 --> 00:58:47.130
is basically L1 estimation.

00:58:47.130 --> 00:58:50.550
By the way, we can actually
now figure something out.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:54.210
We can understand robustness now
from two completely different

00:58:54.210 --> 00:58:55.590
things.

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:59.210
Earlier today, I was
just drawing down--

00:58:59.210 --> 00:59:02.510
let's do the-- let's compare
the square and the quadratic,

00:59:02.510 --> 00:59:03.010
right?

00:59:03.010 --> 00:59:07.720
So the square penalty
looks like this,

00:59:07.720 --> 00:59:10.690
and then the absolute
value looks like that.

00:59:13.740 --> 00:59:16.920
We'd say if you're,
again, not-- if you're not

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:18.552
doing a statistical
interpretation,

00:59:18.552 --> 00:59:20.010
you would say,
well, the difference

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:23.010
between least squares and
L1 estimation-- there's two,

00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:24.000
really.

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:29.460
One, let's focus on
the large residuals.

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:30.873
You'd say, well,
least squares is

00:59:30.873 --> 00:59:32.790
going to be very sensitive
to large residuals.

00:59:32.790 --> 00:59:35.910
Like, it really doesn't like
him because it squares them.

00:59:35.910 --> 00:59:38.880
And you'd say, by contrast, L1
is going to be much more chill.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:40.760
Everybody got what I'm saying?

00:59:40.760 --> 00:59:42.510
And so you'd say, and
what does that mean?

00:59:42.510 --> 00:59:44.310
And you go, well,
what it means is

00:59:44.310 --> 00:59:48.300
that when you do L1
estimation, it's much less

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:50.610
sensitive to outliers.

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:51.850
That's what it means.

00:59:51.850 --> 00:59:54.390
So it's a robust estimator,
actually, literally

00:59:54.390 --> 00:59:55.920
in the statistics sense.

00:59:55.920 --> 00:59:57.090
So it's a robust estimator.

00:59:57.090 --> 00:59:59.670
OK.

00:59:59.670 --> 01:00:02.400
What's cool is you get the
same-- you can argue it here.

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:07.080
You could say, well, the
difference between L2 fitting

01:00:07.080 --> 01:00:10.680
and L1 approximation-- you
say the difference is actually

01:00:10.680 --> 01:00:11.818
basically this.

01:00:11.818 --> 01:00:13.360
It's a difference
between whether you

01:00:13.360 --> 01:00:16.960
believe the noise
density looks like this

01:00:16.960 --> 01:00:20.230
or it looks like this.

01:00:20.230 --> 01:00:22.190
That's a pretty bad job.

01:00:22.190 --> 01:00:25.180
It's a horrible Gaussian,
but you get the idea.

01:00:25.180 --> 01:00:26.410
So it looks like that.

01:00:26.410 --> 01:00:30.610
And actually, you pick
everything off from this.

01:00:30.610 --> 01:00:33.460
You would say,
look, if you thought

01:00:33.460 --> 01:00:37.780
your noises were Laplacian, then
the probability of a 3 sigma

01:00:37.780 --> 01:00:40.330
event is not insignificant.

01:00:40.330 --> 01:00:42.040
I mean, you could
expect to have thing--

01:00:42.040 --> 01:00:43.550
you could expect
to have a noise,

01:00:43.550 --> 01:00:46.150
which is like three times
the standard deviation--

01:00:46.150 --> 01:00:47.530
that's the whole point--

01:00:47.530 --> 01:00:50.120
or four times, or
something in there.

01:00:50.120 --> 01:00:50.860
It can happen.

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:52.053
Gaussian can't happen.

01:00:52.053 --> 01:00:52.720
I mean, kind of.

01:00:52.720 --> 01:00:53.860
You what I mean?

01:00:53.860 --> 01:00:55.450
It's extremely unlikely.

01:00:55.450 --> 01:00:58.870
So you'd say-- so if I
were you, your estimation--

01:00:58.870 --> 01:01:02.530
if it's going to work well, it
cannot be sensitive to these

01:01:02.530 --> 01:01:03.700
large noises.

01:01:03.700 --> 01:01:04.990
Everybody following this?

01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:07.010
So that's why.

01:01:07.010 --> 01:01:10.160
So that explains
why L1 estimation

01:01:10.160 --> 01:01:14.090
would be actually more
robust than the other one

01:01:14.090 --> 01:01:16.940
because you're basically
fitting with the presumption

01:01:16.940 --> 01:01:19.295
that your noises have fat tails.

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:23.750
Oh, and this is actually
interesting, too.

01:01:23.750 --> 01:01:26.330
This is not the main point,
but if you look up here,

01:01:26.330 --> 01:01:28.790
it's actually super interesting.

01:01:28.790 --> 01:01:31.670
So if you zoom in
on a Gaussian, it's

01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:33.560
uniform density at the top.

01:01:33.560 --> 01:01:35.150
it just looks-- it's uniform.

01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:38.165
And you're saying, well,
what that means is,

01:01:38.165 --> 01:01:41.630
I don't-- know it means once
your residual is smaller than

01:01:41.630 --> 01:01:44.180
when you zoom in and a
Gaussian looks like it's flat,

01:01:44.180 --> 01:01:47.900
once your residual is in there,
you don't care if it's 0.01

01:01:47.900 --> 01:01:50.390
or minus 0.03, you
couldn't care less.

01:01:50.390 --> 01:01:52.640
They're all equally likely--
statistically speaking,

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:55.640
they're all reason--
everybody see what I'm saying?

01:01:55.640 --> 01:02:00.920
For a Laplacian noise,
that's not the case.

01:02:00.920 --> 01:02:03.530
As a matter of fact,
there's a sharp peak.

01:02:03.530 --> 01:02:06.420
And actually being 0 is
actually quite likely.

01:02:06.420 --> 01:02:09.190
And that explains also why
you get the sparsity and stuff

01:02:09.190 --> 01:02:09.690
like that.

01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:10.530
But, OK.

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:12.330
So this all makes sense?

01:02:12.330 --> 01:02:14.155
And let's do one
more just for fun.

01:02:14.155 --> 01:02:16.030
And you can turn this
backwards and forwards,

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:18.960
so let's do one more.

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:21.760
You tell me--
suppose someone says,

01:02:21.760 --> 01:02:23.430
I'm going to use Huber fitting.

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:24.430
That looks like this.

01:02:24.430 --> 01:02:26.730
It's quadratic up
to some threshold,

01:02:26.730 --> 01:02:28.740
and then it goes linear, OK?

01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:31.052
So there's Huber fitting.

01:02:31.052 --> 01:02:32.760
And someone says, why
are you doing that?

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:34.740
And you go, well,
because it's, like, I

01:02:34.740 --> 01:02:37.680
want to do least squares,
but I want to be--

01:02:37.680 --> 01:02:40.560
I want to be insensitive
to occasional, weird, large

01:02:40.560 --> 01:02:41.850
noises.

01:02:41.850 --> 01:02:44.850
Done-- that's more than a
good enough explanation.

01:02:44.850 --> 01:02:47.442
But suppose you want to
defend it statistically.

01:02:47.442 --> 01:02:48.900
In fact, how would
you describe it?

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:50.640
How would you say-- what
is Huber fitting when

01:02:50.640 --> 01:02:51.890
you're doing it statistically?

01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:54.420
How do you-- what is it in
terms of maximum likelihood?

01:02:54.420 --> 01:02:56.355
How do you translate
it to that dialect?

01:03:00.010 --> 01:03:02.830
What's Hubert fitting with--

01:03:02.830 --> 01:03:06.610
I mean, what maximum likelihood
problem does it correspond to?

01:03:11.630 --> 01:03:14.160
This is like a mix of the two?

01:03:14.160 --> 01:03:15.870
It's not a mixture.

01:03:15.870 --> 01:03:17.550
It's close.

01:03:17.550 --> 01:03:20.020
It's actually pretty close
to a mixture, but it's not.

01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:22.500
It's actually a weird--
it says, oh, I'm doing

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:23.760
maximum likelihood estimation.

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:26.490
You go, with what noise density?

01:03:26.490 --> 01:03:29.340
And they would say, well,
it's this weird Frankenstein

01:03:29.340 --> 01:03:32.850
density that looks like this.

01:03:32.850 --> 01:03:33.880
It looks like this.

01:03:33.880 --> 01:03:35.700
It's a Gaussian.

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:38.130
it's actually E
to the minus this.

01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:43.020
So it's actually, literally,
exactly a Gaussian out

01:03:43.020 --> 01:03:44.370
to some threshold.

01:03:44.370 --> 01:03:49.308
And then, you glue on to
it an exponential tail.

01:03:49.308 --> 01:03:50.100
Everybody got that?

01:03:50.100 --> 01:03:52.800
Because that's what the x of
the Huber thing is, right?

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.992
You have to normalize
it, but who cares?

01:03:54.992 --> 01:03:55.950
But it looks like that.

01:03:55.950 --> 01:03:56.678
Everyone agree?

01:03:56.678 --> 01:03:58.470
And someone said, well,
what are you doing?

01:03:58.470 --> 01:04:01.260
And you go, I'm doing maximum
likelihood estimation.

01:04:01.260 --> 01:04:03.807
And you go, then why aren't
you using-- it and you say,

01:04:03.807 --> 01:04:06.390
it's pretty much Gaussian, but
I'm going to use Huber instead.

01:04:06.390 --> 01:04:07.985
And they'd say,
what does that mean?

01:04:07.985 --> 01:04:09.360
And you say, well,
what it really

01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:14.180
means is I'm assuming that
my noise density, this thing

01:04:14.180 --> 01:04:15.063
would go way down.

01:04:15.063 --> 01:04:17.480
I mean, I'd have to adjust
things to get the normalization

01:04:17.480 --> 01:04:18.650
right, but whatever.

01:04:18.650 --> 01:04:20.550
The density is
proportional to this.

01:04:20.550 --> 01:04:22.670
So what you do is you
just take a Gaussian,

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:26.750
cut it, and then glue
on exponential tails.

01:04:26.750 --> 01:04:29.180
If you do maximum likelihood
estimation, congratulations,

01:04:29.180 --> 01:04:32.320
you're doing Huber fitting.

01:04:32.320 --> 01:04:33.650
Everybody got this?

01:04:33.650 --> 01:04:34.370
Makes sense?

01:04:34.370 --> 01:04:36.170
So there's lots of
ways to understand

01:04:36.170 --> 01:04:39.350
exactly the same thing.

01:04:39.350 --> 01:04:41.840
What about-- what if I did--

01:04:41.840 --> 01:04:46.820
what if I did fitting
with a, I don't know, here

01:04:46.820 --> 01:04:49.325
with a dead zone linear penalty?

01:04:52.580 --> 01:04:54.640
That's maximum
likelihood for what?

01:05:00.106 --> 01:05:02.150
What's your noise
density for that?

01:05:07.070 --> 01:05:09.185
Yeah.

01:05:09.185 --> 01:05:10.690
You say, well, my noise is this.

01:05:10.690 --> 01:05:13.660
It's actually flat--
it's a uniform.

01:05:13.660 --> 01:05:15.670
It's a uniform
over some interval.

01:05:15.670 --> 01:05:18.610
And then that's some
fraction of the probability.

01:05:18.610 --> 01:05:19.810
And then I glue on exp--

01:05:19.810 --> 01:05:21.700
I glue on exponential tails.

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:25.540
So you'd say actually,
between plus and minus 0.1,

01:05:25.540 --> 01:05:26.470
it's just uniform.

01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:28.900
I mean, you're equally likely
to have anything in there

01:05:28.900 --> 01:05:29.783
in the noise.

01:05:29.783 --> 01:05:31.450
And you'd say,
occasionally, though, you

01:05:31.450 --> 01:05:34.450
get some big things, and those
are these exponential tails

01:05:34.450 --> 01:05:36.900
you glued on.

01:05:36.900 --> 01:05:38.480
All make sense?

01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:41.320
So, OK.

01:05:41.320 --> 01:05:42.040
OK.

01:05:42.040 --> 01:05:45.350
We'll look at just
a couple of others.

01:05:45.350 --> 01:05:49.330
So, logistic regression, which
I very much hope many people

01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:49.840
have seen.

01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:51.700
But anyway.

01:05:51.700 --> 01:05:53.620
And it's the same sort of thing.

01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:57.910
You would say I have a--

01:05:57.910 --> 01:06:02.770
here I have a random
variable, takes values 01.

01:06:02.770 --> 01:06:05.090
And the probability of
being 1 is a logistic

01:06:05.090 --> 01:06:07.940
of this thing, which
is an affine function

01:06:07.940 --> 01:06:10.550
of some features or
explanatory variables

01:06:10.550 --> 01:06:11.870
that you can see right.

01:06:11.870 --> 01:06:16.070
So that's what it would be.

01:06:16.070 --> 01:06:18.400
And so that is the--

01:06:18.400 --> 01:06:23.710
and here you would say something
like, conditioned on you,

01:06:23.710 --> 01:06:27.280
you'd say the density or, sorry,
the probability mass function,

01:06:27.280 --> 01:06:30.050
or just the probability
that it's 1.

01:06:30.050 --> 01:06:33.730
It depends on a parameter
which is A and B.

01:06:33.730 --> 01:06:34.960
If you just take the--

01:06:34.960 --> 01:06:37.722
if you take the log of this,
you end up with this thing,

01:06:37.722 --> 01:06:38.680
and it comes down here.

01:06:38.680 --> 01:06:41.470
And you can see immediately
that this is going to be concave

01:06:41.470 --> 01:06:45.460
because that's affine.

01:06:45.460 --> 01:06:50.200
And then this is log sum exp
because this is exp of 0.

01:06:50.200 --> 01:06:53.320
So this is log sum exp.

01:06:53.320 --> 01:06:55.300
I think that's
the logistic loss.

01:06:55.300 --> 01:06:58.000
And with a minus sign,
it becomes concave.

01:06:58.000 --> 01:07:00.010
And affine is concave.

01:07:00.010 --> 01:07:02.230
So that's a--

01:07:02.230 --> 01:07:04.030
I'm sure people have seen this.

01:07:04.030 --> 01:07:06.400
so that's the idea.

01:07:06.400 --> 01:07:09.520
So that's logistic modeling.

01:07:09.520 --> 01:07:12.440
And then this would just be
like a little baby example.

01:07:12.440 --> 01:07:12.940
so.

01:07:12.940 --> 01:07:17.360
The example is here I have
one explanatory variable U,

01:07:17.360 --> 01:07:19.250
which might be age or--

01:07:19.250 --> 01:07:21.560
I don't know, I'm just
making this up, age.

01:07:21.560 --> 01:07:23.780
And this is the, I don't
know, the probability

01:07:23.780 --> 01:07:28.127
that when you're admitted
for something to the ER,

01:07:28.127 --> 01:07:29.210
I don't know, you survive.

01:07:29.210 --> 01:07:30.543
I mean, I'm just making this up.

01:07:30.543 --> 01:07:32.837
But that would be it.

01:07:32.837 --> 01:07:34.920
Oh, it looks like young
people don't do very well,

01:07:34.920 --> 01:07:36.253
and older people do pretty well.

01:07:36.253 --> 01:07:37.250
Maybe this is the--

01:07:37.250 --> 01:07:39.655
maybe that's the probability
that you don't survive.

01:07:39.655 --> 01:07:40.280
How about that?

01:07:40.280 --> 01:07:41.600
And then this makes sense.

01:07:41.600 --> 01:07:43.940
Then it has the right flavor.

01:07:43.940 --> 01:07:45.800
So, OK.

01:07:45.800 --> 01:07:48.680
And so these dots
are your sample--

01:07:48.680 --> 01:07:49.940
the data you have.

01:07:49.940 --> 01:07:54.180
You'd say, here's someone
who was 63, didn't survive.

01:07:54.180 --> 01:07:57.350
Here's someone who was 27,
did survive or something

01:07:57.350 --> 01:07:58.580
like, that kind of thing.

01:07:58.580 --> 01:07:59.450
That's the data.

01:07:59.450 --> 01:08:01.310
And your eyeball
would look at it

01:08:01.310 --> 01:08:04.850
and say, look, the
probability of being 1

01:08:04.850 --> 01:08:06.950
is higher when U is large.

01:08:06.950 --> 01:08:09.560
But there's things
that are not obvious.

01:08:09.560 --> 01:08:11.480
Like, how about there?

01:08:11.480 --> 01:08:13.190
There's someone,
like, really old.

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:14.902
And for whatever
reason, they survived,

01:08:14.902 --> 01:08:15.860
or something like that.

01:08:15.860 --> 01:08:17.450
I'm just making this up.

01:08:17.450 --> 01:08:19.460
OK.

01:08:19.460 --> 01:08:20.660
And if you fit--

01:08:20.660 --> 01:08:22.970
now, that's just a
convex problem to fit it.

01:08:22.970 --> 01:08:26.090
And you would fit
it, and you would--

01:08:26.090 --> 01:08:28.640
you'd end up with
this curve here.

01:08:28.640 --> 01:08:31.370
So it looks like
that, this curve here.

01:08:31.370 --> 01:08:32.819
It kind of makes sense.

01:08:32.819 --> 01:08:36.060
Let me ask a couple of
questions about this.

01:08:36.060 --> 01:08:38.449
What if the data actually
looked like this?

01:08:42.450 --> 01:08:44.130
What if the data
looked like this?

01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:45.020
Here's you.

01:08:45.020 --> 01:08:47.630
And suppose it looked like--
here's 1 and here's 0.

01:08:47.630 --> 01:08:50.023
And suppose there's a whole
bunch of points along here,

01:08:50.023 --> 01:08:52.190
and then there's a whole
bunch of points along here.

01:08:57.529 --> 01:09:00.729
So a couple of questions--
what does it mean?

01:09:00.729 --> 01:09:03.490
Tell me about the maximum
likelihood problem.

01:09:03.490 --> 01:09:05.330
And let's just talk about that.

01:09:05.330 --> 01:09:06.970
So what does it mean?

01:09:12.729 --> 01:09:15.810
This just means, look,
everyone less than 32 survived.

01:09:15.810 --> 01:09:18.180
No one above that
age survived, OK?

01:09:18.180 --> 01:09:21.885
So you tell me
what does it mean?

01:09:21.885 --> 01:09:24.510
I mean, there's so many ways to
say this in different dialects.

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:25.814
It's-- yeah.

01:09:25.814 --> 01:09:26.939
They're linearly separable.

01:09:26.939 --> 01:09:27.439
Yeah.

01:09:27.439 --> 01:09:30.569
You would say that the data
are linearly separable.

01:09:30.569 --> 01:09:31.620
OK.

01:09:31.620 --> 01:09:35.729
How about the-- what
about the logistic?

01:09:35.729 --> 01:09:38.609
Sorry, the log likelihood?

01:09:38.609 --> 01:09:41.250
Suppose I asked you to-- suppose
I ask you to maximize the log

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:42.990
likelihood here.

01:09:42.990 --> 01:09:45.137
Again, you can just
guess, or you might know.

01:09:45.137 --> 01:09:46.845
But what do you think
is going to happen?

01:09:55.310 --> 01:09:57.510
Any guesses?

01:09:57.510 --> 01:10:01.700
How should I draw your actual--

01:10:01.700 --> 01:10:03.377
I'll draw the logistic
map corresponding

01:10:03.377 --> 01:10:04.460
to the maximum likelihood.

01:10:04.460 --> 01:10:07.760
What's it going to look like?

01:10:07.760 --> 01:10:08.860
Yeah, OK.

01:10:08.860 --> 01:10:11.650
I'll take your-- it's
going to look like this.

01:10:11.650 --> 01:10:14.290
It's going to have a super
sharp like that, right?

01:10:14.290 --> 01:10:14.920
OK.

01:10:14.920 --> 01:10:17.830
How sharp is this going to be?

01:10:17.830 --> 01:10:21.220
Infinitely sharp because the
less sharp you make this,

01:10:21.220 --> 01:10:22.930
you start dropping
the probability here.

01:10:22.930 --> 01:10:25.090
But you know
everyone over age 33

01:10:25.090 --> 01:10:29.440
survived, so you're losing
log likelihood, right?

01:10:29.440 --> 01:10:31.120
And the other thing,
too, is if you

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:34.570
make this less than
infinite slope here,

01:10:34.570 --> 01:10:39.040
then you're shortchanging
the predictions here.

01:10:39.040 --> 01:10:40.900
So in this case,
this is precisely

01:10:40.900 --> 01:10:47.100
the case when the log
likelihood is unbounded above.

01:10:47.100 --> 01:10:49.650
So this is extremely
common in models.

01:10:49.650 --> 01:10:51.750
If the log likelihood
is unbounded above,

01:10:51.750 --> 01:10:54.450
it means they're separable
and stuff like that.

01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:56.790
So, of course, this is fixed
by regression and things

01:10:56.790 --> 01:10:57.790
like that.

01:10:57.790 --> 01:10:59.490
Sorry, by adding regularization.

01:10:59.490 --> 01:11:02.040
So that's fine.

01:11:02.040 --> 01:11:04.100
OK.

01:11:04.100 --> 01:11:07.670
All right, our last
topic is just a sort

01:11:07.670 --> 01:11:08.990
of a whirlwind of topics.

01:11:08.990 --> 01:11:12.545
There's more in the book, which
we expect you to be reading.

01:11:15.050 --> 01:11:20.210
And the last one is just on kind
of basic hypothesis testing.

01:11:20.210 --> 01:11:22.610
And the setting here,
it's super simple setting.

01:11:22.610 --> 01:11:26.010
It just says I have
a random variable.

01:11:26.010 --> 01:11:29.105
And for fun, I'm just going to
make it with values in 1 to n.

01:11:31.615 --> 01:11:34.670
And it was generated
from two different-- one

01:11:34.670 --> 01:11:36.170
of two different distributions.

01:11:36.170 --> 01:11:39.818
And you have to guess which
one it was generated from.

01:11:39.818 --> 01:11:40.610
Did it come from P?

01:11:40.610 --> 01:11:41.480
Did it come from Q?

01:11:41.480 --> 01:11:43.640
That's your question.

01:11:43.640 --> 01:11:46.730
I mean, it's the
basic question here.

01:11:46.730 --> 01:11:48.960
And the way that's
going to work is

01:11:48.960 --> 01:11:50.210
an outcome is going to happen.

01:11:50.210 --> 01:11:54.680
Like, I'm going to
say, OK, I observe 7.

01:11:54.680 --> 01:11:59.528
And then from that,
you have to guess Was.

01:11:59.528 --> 01:12:01.820
It from distribution P or Q?

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:03.890
You could almost guess.

01:12:03.890 --> 01:12:05.930
Or from a course,
you would certainly

01:12:05.930 --> 01:12:11.090
know what the optimal guess is.

01:12:11.090 --> 01:12:17.300
I mean, it'd be very weird if
P7 was like 0.8 or something

01:12:17.300 --> 01:12:19.910
and Q7 was 0.05.

01:12:19.910 --> 01:12:21.970
And I said 7 is what occurred.

01:12:21.970 --> 01:12:23.470
Which one do you
think it came from?

01:12:23.470 --> 01:12:25.403
And you go, oh, Q.

01:12:25.403 --> 01:12:27.570
It's just-- I mean, if you
think carefully about it,

01:12:27.570 --> 01:12:28.320
that's weird.

01:12:28.320 --> 01:12:29.210
I mean, unless
you know something

01:12:29.210 --> 01:12:31.293
you secretly behind the--
you must know something.

01:12:31.293 --> 01:12:35.180
And you go, whoa, it's much
more likely with the first model

01:12:35.180 --> 01:12:35.967
than the second.

01:12:35.967 --> 01:12:38.300
And you go, yeah, but I've
been doing this for 20 years.

01:12:38.300 --> 01:12:39.870
I got a good feel
for these things.

01:12:39.870 --> 01:12:43.010
So go with Q. I mean,
anyway, I'm just

01:12:43.010 --> 01:12:45.320
saying you can even
guess what it is,

01:12:45.320 --> 01:12:47.120
but we'll just work
out what it is.

01:12:47.120 --> 01:12:48.063
OK.

01:12:48.063 --> 01:12:49.480
So what we're going
to do is we're

01:12:49.480 --> 01:12:52.130
going to look at a
randomized detector.

01:12:52.130 --> 01:12:54.830
And a randomized detector
is like a 2 by n matrix.

01:12:54.830 --> 01:12:56.840
It's a very weird thing.

01:12:56.840 --> 01:12:58.160
It looks like this.

01:12:58.160 --> 01:12:59.390
So it's a 2 by n matrix.

01:12:59.390 --> 01:13:04.340
It looks like 0.8,
0.2, 1, 0, 0, 1.

01:13:04.340 --> 01:13:05.060
That's good.

01:13:05.060 --> 01:13:07.850
And 0.3 and 0.7.

01:13:07.850 --> 01:13:12.900
And let's say this is P and
Q, and this is the outcome.

01:13:12.900 --> 01:13:15.460
This is 1 and that's n.

01:13:15.460 --> 01:13:18.470
So the idea here is
this is my detector.

01:13:18.470 --> 01:13:19.220
It's a policy.

01:13:19.220 --> 01:13:23.210
And what it does is it says
that if the outcome is 2,

01:13:23.210 --> 01:13:27.090
I will simply say it
came from P. Period.

01:13:27.090 --> 01:13:28.800
Everybody following this?

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:31.050
If the outcome is
3, I'll say it came

01:13:31.050 --> 01:13:35.680
from Q. If the outcome
is 1, I will then

01:13:35.680 --> 01:13:37.210
do something very weird.

01:13:37.210 --> 01:13:40.070
I'll flip a coin
with probability 0.8.

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:42.850
And if it comes up heads,
I'll say it's P. Otherwise,

01:13:42.850 --> 01:13:46.795
I'll say it's Q.
everybody got that?

01:13:46.795 --> 01:13:48.670
By the way, we're going
to see examples where

01:13:48.670 --> 01:13:50.380
you need a randomized detector.

01:13:50.380 --> 01:13:53.170
Oh, a deterministic detector
is when all the numbers

01:13:53.170 --> 01:13:54.610
in that matrix are 0 or 1.

01:13:54.610 --> 01:13:58.180
And then it's just a partition
of the outcomes, which

01:13:58.180 --> 01:14:01.540
are 1 to n into the ones
where you guess it's P,

01:14:01.540 --> 01:14:04.420
and the ones where
you guess it's Q Yeah.

01:14:04.420 --> 01:14:07.540
Isn't P a distribution
and Q is a distribution?

01:14:07.540 --> 01:14:09.130
Yeah.

01:14:09.130 --> 01:14:11.560
Those are n numbers that are
non-negative and add up to 1.

01:14:11.560 --> 01:14:12.040
Yeah.

01:14:12.040 --> 01:14:13.915
But like, here they
don't add up to 1, right?

01:14:13.915 --> 01:14:16.300
This is a detector matrix.

01:14:16.300 --> 01:14:20.020
This is not a probability--
this is not a probability

01:14:20.020 --> 01:14:21.550
distribution at all.

01:14:21.550 --> 01:14:22.180
Yeah.

01:14:22.180 --> 01:14:24.070
So P and Q do.

01:14:24.070 --> 01:14:25.690
Those things, of course, right?

01:14:25.690 --> 01:14:27.070
The sum of the Pi's is 1.

01:14:27.070 --> 01:14:29.770
Sum of the Qi's is 1, right?

01:14:29.770 --> 01:14:32.950
The only thing-- in fact,
the rules for this matrix

01:14:32.950 --> 01:14:37.770
are they're non-negative,
and the column sums are 1.

01:14:37.770 --> 01:14:40.200
That's the rules for
this detector matrix.

01:14:40.200 --> 01:14:41.940
It's a randomized--
it characterizes

01:14:41.940 --> 01:14:43.230
a randomized detector.

01:14:43.230 --> 01:14:45.630
So you'd say it's like column
stochastic or something

01:14:45.630 --> 01:14:47.220
like that, the matrix.

01:14:51.270 --> 01:14:52.910
OK.

01:14:52.910 --> 01:14:57.740
Now, what you could do
is just work out the--

01:14:57.740 --> 01:15:01.130
you can actually just work out
the so-called detector matrix,

01:15:01.130 --> 01:15:03.230
which actually you get
by just doing literally

01:15:03.230 --> 01:15:04.970
matrix multiplication.

01:15:04.970 --> 01:15:08.180
So if I do the following--

01:15:08.180 --> 01:15:12.410
if I multiply my
matrix here by--

01:15:12.410 --> 01:15:15.350
here's p and here's Q--

01:15:15.350 --> 01:15:18.140
I get a 2 by 2 matrix,
which is super interesting.

01:15:18.140 --> 01:15:21.800
And what it's going to
tell me-- it's called,

01:15:21.800 --> 01:15:24.740
I don't know, confusion matrix
or lots of names for this.

01:15:24.740 --> 01:15:28.010
But it tells you actually
what the probability

01:15:28.010 --> 01:15:29.960
of your being either
right or wrong

01:15:29.960 --> 01:15:37.100
is as a function of whether
it was generated from P or Q.

01:15:37.100 --> 01:15:40.640
And so that's called a confusion
matrix, although maybe for a 2

01:15:40.640 --> 01:15:41.360
by 2 it's not.

01:15:41.360 --> 01:15:42.740
But I don't know,
it's got some--

01:15:42.740 --> 01:15:43.760
I don't know what it is.

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:46.280
Anyway.

01:15:46.280 --> 01:15:48.890
And so here you would like the--

01:15:48.890 --> 01:15:50.630
if this were the--
if this matrix,

01:15:50.630 --> 01:15:52.590
this detection
probability matrix

01:15:52.590 --> 01:15:56.400
were an identity, that's perfect
because it means that whenever

01:15:56.400 --> 01:16:00.090
it comes from P, I guess P.
Whenever it comes from Q,

01:16:00.090 --> 01:16:00.915
I guess Q.

01:16:00.915 --> 01:16:02.820
By the way, would that ever ha--

01:16:02.820 --> 01:16:04.440
when do you imagine
that's possible?

01:16:04.440 --> 01:16:05.607
Somebody give me an example.

01:16:09.270 --> 01:16:09.770
yeah.

01:16:09.770 --> 01:16:11.340
When the distributions
don't overlap.

01:16:11.340 --> 01:16:11.840
Yeah.

01:16:11.840 --> 01:16:12.890
They don't overlap.

01:16:12.890 --> 01:16:16.280
So here's P. I'll draw a P here.

01:16:16.280 --> 01:16:19.065
It's 0, 0, 0.5.

01:16:19.065 --> 01:16:20.000
0.5.

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:22.310
And here's Q. This
is P and here's

01:16:22.310 --> 01:16:27.350
Q. It's going to be 0.03, OK?

01:16:27.350 --> 01:16:29.697
So they just have
non-overlapping support,

01:16:29.697 --> 01:16:30.780
or they're just different.

01:16:30.780 --> 01:16:32.570
And so this is silly.

01:16:32.570 --> 01:16:38.500
But now, if I tell you the
outcome is 3, what can you say?

01:16:38.500 --> 01:16:42.370
And with certainty, you can
say, if the outcome is 3,

01:16:42.370 --> 01:16:44.860
it can't have come from
Q, so it came from P.

01:16:44.860 --> 01:16:49.540
And if the outcome were 2,
it came from Q, not P, right?

01:16:49.540 --> 01:16:52.600
And so in this case, if you
had a reasonable deterministic

01:16:52.600 --> 01:16:54.820
detector there,
you would come up

01:16:54.820 --> 01:16:58.330
with this detection
matrix being the identity,

01:16:58.330 --> 01:17:01.300
meaning you make no mistakes.

01:17:01.300 --> 01:17:04.450
I mean, that's a silly thing,
but nevertheless, that's

01:17:04.450 --> 01:17:05.020
the idea.

01:17:05.020 --> 01:17:07.330
OK.

01:17:07.330 --> 01:17:11.500
So what you really end up having
in the choice of T now is this

01:17:11.500 --> 01:17:17.350
is a multicriterion problem
because there's two-- actually,

01:17:17.350 --> 01:17:20.410
you can even weirdly say that--

01:17:20.410 --> 01:17:22.090
well, here we can--

01:17:22.090 --> 01:17:24.405
this matrix only has two
free parameters in it--

01:17:24.405 --> 01:17:26.530
the probability of a false
negative and probability

01:17:26.530 --> 01:17:27.340
of false positive.

01:17:27.340 --> 01:17:29.950
Both of those you want small.

01:17:29.950 --> 01:17:31.930
So we can say it's a
bicriterion problem,

01:17:31.930 --> 01:17:33.400
and it would look like this.

01:17:33.400 --> 01:17:34.970
It looks exactly like this.

01:17:34.970 --> 01:17:40.180
So, yeah, this is
what you would do.

01:17:40.180 --> 01:17:41.950
These are your false
probability and

01:17:41.950 --> 01:17:44.710
false negative-- false positive
false negative probabilities.

01:17:44.710 --> 01:17:46.300
You want them both small.

01:17:46.300 --> 01:17:47.562
So what's cool is over here--

01:17:47.562 --> 01:17:49.520
I mean, with this ridiculous
example over here,

01:17:49.520 --> 01:17:51.400
we can make both of them 0.

01:17:51.400 --> 01:17:53.770
But in general, obviously,
you can't do that.

01:17:53.770 --> 01:17:57.970
so it's a bicriterion problem,
and it looks like this.

01:17:57.970 --> 01:18:02.320
That is an LP, right?

01:18:02.320 --> 01:18:04.450
Actually, it's an
LP that's so simple,

01:18:04.450 --> 01:18:10.180
it has an analytical solution,
which you could work out.

01:18:10.180 --> 01:18:12.110
We'll get to that in a minute.

01:18:12.110 --> 01:18:14.620
But if you scalerize
it-- and so lambda

01:18:14.620 --> 01:18:18.100
would be the penalty
that tells you

01:18:18.100 --> 01:18:24.220
how much false negatives bother
you more than false positives.

01:18:24.220 --> 01:18:26.050
If you solve this,
you actually end up

01:18:26.050 --> 01:18:30.130
with a solution that is
a deterministic detector.

01:18:30.130 --> 01:18:33.310
So you get-- and as
a matter of fact,

01:18:33.310 --> 01:18:35.240
the method is actually
incredibly simple.

01:18:35.240 --> 01:18:36.280
It's really dumb.

01:18:36.280 --> 01:18:40.390
It basically says that it
depends on the ratio of P

01:18:40.390 --> 01:18:45.010
over Q. And so this
is, I guess, they

01:18:45.010 --> 01:18:47.680
call it a likelihood ratio test.

01:18:47.680 --> 01:18:49.763
I mean, this is a super
dumb example, but actually

01:18:49.763 --> 01:18:51.472
this kind of works in
infinite dimensions

01:18:51.472 --> 01:18:52.360
and things like that.

01:18:52.360 --> 01:18:55.150
So it'd be the exact same
thing in infinite dimensions.

01:18:55.150 --> 01:18:57.550
If I had two
continuous densities,

01:18:57.550 --> 01:19:02.290
and I say, hey, here's x,
which density did it come from?

01:19:02.290 --> 01:19:03.997
You look at the
two-- what you do

01:19:03.997 --> 01:19:05.830
is you'd look at the
ratio of the densities.

01:19:05.830 --> 01:19:07.630
And then there's
a threshold, which

01:19:07.630 --> 01:19:09.950
you interpret as
how much you care

01:19:09.950 --> 01:19:12.763
about being false negative
compared to false positive.

01:19:12.763 --> 01:19:14.180
And if you're above
the threshold,

01:19:14.180 --> 01:19:15.830
you guess one distribution
if you're below and all

01:19:15.830 --> 01:19:16.370
that kind of stuff.

01:19:16.370 --> 01:19:16.870
OK.

01:19:16.870 --> 01:19:19.080
Everybody got this?

01:19:19.080 --> 01:19:20.910
So, OK.

01:19:20.910 --> 01:19:25.500
Now, those are
deterministic detectors.

01:19:25.500 --> 01:19:27.150
But you could do other stuff.

01:19:27.150 --> 01:19:30.400
Like, you could say, actually,
here's what I care about--

01:19:30.400 --> 01:19:34.290
I want to minimize the
probability of being wrong.

01:19:34.290 --> 01:19:35.670
Minimize the probability.

01:19:35.670 --> 01:19:36.570
Did I say maximize?

01:19:36.570 --> 01:19:37.110
Whatever.

01:19:37.110 --> 01:19:41.670
You want to minimize the
probability of being wrong.

01:19:41.670 --> 01:19:45.030
And so there, you're minimizing
the max of these two things.

01:19:45.030 --> 01:19:48.330
That's an LP, and
it usually ends up

01:19:48.330 --> 01:19:51.330
not being a
deterministic detector.

01:19:51.330 --> 01:19:58.700
So we'll quit today, and we'll
start up again next time.

